window.CHAPTER_DATA = {
  "1": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch1\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">1</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">Science • Class 10 Board Exam (NCERT Rationalized)</div>\n      <h2>Chapter 1: Chemical Reactions and Equations</h2>\n      <p>Balancing Equations, Types of Reactions (Combination, Decomposition, Displacement, Redox) & Everyday Effects — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text Questions (Page 6, 10, 13)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch1-q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch1-q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why should a magnesium ribbon be cleaned before burning in air?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Scientific Reason:</strong></p>\n<p>Magnesium is a highly reactive metal. When exposed to atmospheric air, it slowly reacts with atmospheric oxygen to form a thin, stable, and protective inert layer of <strong>basic magnesium oxide ($MgO$)</strong> and basic magnesium carbonate on its surface:</p>\n$$2\\text{Mg}(s) + \\text{O}_2(g) \\longrightarrow 2\\text{MgO}(s)$$\n<p>This protective oxide coating prevents further contact of the underlying magnesium metal with oxygen and inhibits ignition. Therefore, before burning in air, the ribbon must be thoroughly rubbed and cleaned with sandpaper to remove this inert oxide layer so that pure magnesium metal comes into direct contact with atmospheric oxygen, allowing it to ignite smoothly with a dazzling white flame.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Explaining formation of basic magnesium oxide (MgO) protective layer</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Stating that sandpaper abrasion exposes fresh reactive Mg metal for smooth combustion</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch1-q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch1-q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Write the balanced equation for the following chemical reactions: (i) Hydrogen + Chlorine → Hydrogen chloride, (ii) Barium chloride + Aluminium sulphate → Barium sulphate + Aluminium chloride, (iii) Sodium + Water → Sodium hydroxide + Hydrogen.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Step-by-step Balanced Chemical Equations with Physical State Symbols:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(i) Hydrogen + Chlorine $\\longrightarrow$ Hydrogen chloride:</strong><br>\n    $$\\text{H}_2(g) + \\text{Cl}_2(g) \\longrightarrow 2\\text{HCl}(g)$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(ii) Barium chloride + Aluminium sulphate $\\longrightarrow$ Barium sulphate + Aluminium chloride:</strong><br>\n    $$3\\text{BaCl}_2(aq) + \\text{Al}_2(\\text{SO}_4)_3(aq) \\longrightarrow 3\\text{BaSO}_4(s)\\downarrow + 2\\text{AlCl}_3(aq)$$\n    <em>Note:</em> Barium sulphate forms a dense white precipitate.\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(iii) Sodium + Water $\\longrightarrow$ Sodium hydroxide + Hydrogen:</strong><br>\n    $$2\\text{Na}(s) + 2\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l) \\longrightarrow 2\\text{NaOH}(aq) + \\text{H}_2(g)\\uparrow + \\text{Heat}$$\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Balanced equation for reaction (i) with state symbols</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Balanced equation for reaction (ii) showing white ppt BaSO4</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Balanced exothermic equation for reaction (iii)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch1-q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch1-q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">A solution of a substance 'X' is used for white-washing. (i) Name the substance 'X' and write its formula. (ii) Write the reaction of the substance 'X' named in (i) above with water.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Identification and Reaction of Substance 'X':</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(i) Name and Formula:</strong><br>\n    The substance 'X' used for white-washing is <strong>Calcium oxide</strong> (commonly called <strong>Quicklime</strong>).<br>\n    <strong>Chemical Formula:</strong> $\\text{CaO}$.\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(ii) Chemical Reaction with Water:</strong><br>\n    Quicklime reacts vigorously with liquid water in a highly exothermic reaction to produce <strong>slaked lime</strong> (calcium hydroxide) with the evolution of a large amount of heat and hissing sound:\n    $$\\text{CaO}(s) + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l) \\longrightarrow \\text{Ca(OH)}_2(aq) + \\text{Heat}$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Application Note:</strong><br>\n    When slaked lime solution is applied to walls, it slowly reacts with carbon dioxide present in the air to form a thin, shiny layer of calcium carbonate ($\\text{CaCO}_3$) over 2 to 3 days:\n    $$\\text{Ca(OH)}_2(aq) + \\text{CO}_2(g) \\longrightarrow \\text{CaCO}_3(s) + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l)$$\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Identifying substance X as calcium oxide (quicklime, CaO)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Balanced equation for reaction with water producing Ca(OH)2 + Heat</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch1-q4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch1-q4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why is the amount of gas collected in one of the test tubes in the electrolysis of water double of the amount collected in the other? Name this gas.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Scientific Explanation:</strong></p>\n<p>In the electrolysis of water, water molecules ($\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$) are decomposed into hydrogen gas and oxygen gas by passing an electric current through acidulated water:</p>\n$$2\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l) \\xrightarrow{\\text{Electric current}} 2\\text{H}_2(g) + \\text{O}_2(g)$$\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Mole Ratio:</strong> According to the balanced chemical equation and Avogadro's Law (equal volumes of gases contain equal numbers of molecules at the same temperature and pressure), <strong>2 moles (or 2 volumes)</strong> of hydrogen gas are liberated for every <strong>1 mole (or 1 volume)</strong> of oxygen gas.</li>\n  <li><strong>Electrode Reactions:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>At Cathode (negative electrode): Hydrogen ions gain electrons: $2\\text{H}^+ + 2e^- \\longrightarrow \\text{H}_2(g)$ [Volume = 2 units].</li>\n      <li>At Anode (positive electrode): Oxygen gas is collected: $2\\text{O}^{2-} \\longrightarrow \\text{O}_2(g) + 4e^-$ [Volume = 1 unit].</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The gas collected in double the volume is <strong>Hydrogen ($\\text{H}_2$)</strong> because water contains hydrogen and oxygen atoms chemically combined in a fixed $2 : 1$ atomic ratio by volume.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Writing balanced electrolysis equation 2H2O -> 2H2 + O2</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Explaining 2:1 volume ratio from stoichiometric coefficients and identifying gas as Hydrogen</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT Exercise Questions & Board Typology</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch1-q5\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch1-q5')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why does the colour of copper sulphate solution change when an iron nail is dipped in it?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Observation and Chemical Mechanism:</strong></p>\n<p>When an iron nail is placed in blue-coloured copper sulphate solution, a single <strong>displacement reaction</strong> occurs because <strong>Iron ($\\text{Fe}$) is more reactive than Copper ($\\text{Cu}$)</strong> in the electrochemical reactivity series.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Chemical Reaction:</strong></p>\n$$\\text{Fe}(s) + \\text{CuSO}_4(aq) \\longrightarrow \\text{FeSO}_4(aq) + \\text{Cu}(s)$$\n$$\\text{(Grey iron nail)} + \\text{(Blue solution)} \\longrightarrow \\text{(Pale green solution)} + \\text{(Reddish-brown deposit)}$$\n\n<p><strong>Observable Changes:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>The characteristic deep <strong>blue colour</strong> of copper sulphate fades gradually and transforms into a <strong>light pale green</strong> solution due to the formation of ferrous sulphate ($\\text{FeSO}_4$).</li>\n  <li>A shining <strong>reddish-brown coating</strong> of displaced metallic copper gets deposited on the surface of the iron nail.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Reason: Iron is more reactive than copper and displaces it</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Balanced chemical equation with physical states</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Detailing color changes: blue to pale green and reddish-brown copper deposit</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch1-q6\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch1-q6')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 6</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why is respiration considered an exothermic reaction? Explain with balanced chemical equation.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Detailed Biochemical Explanation:</strong></p>\n<p>Respiration is considered an <strong>exothermic reaction</strong> because energy is released during the metabolic breakdown of food inside living cells.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Mechanistic Steps:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>During digestion, carbohydrates consumed in our diet (such as rice, potatoes, bread) are broken down into simpler sugars, predominantly <strong>glucose ($\\text{C}_6\\text{H}_{12}\\text{O}_6$)</strong>.</li>\n  <li>This glucose is absorbed into the bloodstream and delivered to tissue cells where it combines with inhaled oxygen in the presence of cellular enzymes.</li>\n  <li>The glucose undergoes slow, controlled combustion (cellular respiration) to form carbon dioxide, water, and releases metabolic energy in the form of <strong>ATP (Adenosine Triphosphate)</strong>:\n    $$\\text{C}_6\\text{H}_{12}\\text{O}_6(aq) + 6\\text{O}_2(aq) \\longrightarrow 6\\text{CO}_2(aq) + 6\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l) + \\text{Energy (38 ATP)}$$\n  </li>\n  <li>Since substantial energy is liberated to maintain vital cellular functions and body temperature, cellular respiration is classified as an <strong>exothermic process</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Explaining breakdown of carbohydrates into glucose during digestion</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Writing balanced chemical equation for cellular respiration</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Deducing exothermic nature from net liberation of energy/ATP</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch1-q7\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch1-q7')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 7</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why are decomposition reactions called the opposite of combination reactions? Write equations for decomposition reactions where energy is supplied in the form of heat, light and electricity.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[5 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Fundamental Comparison:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>A <strong>Combination reaction</strong> is a chemical reaction in which two or more simple reactants (elements or compounds) combine together to form a single, more complex product:\n    $$A + B \\longrightarrow AB + \\text{Heat (usually exothermic)}$$\n  </li>\n  <li>A <strong>Decomposition reaction</strong> is a chemical reaction in which a single complex reactant breaks down into two or more simpler substances upon the absorption of external energy (heat, light, or electricity):\n    $$AB + \\text{Energy} \\longrightarrow A + B \\text{ (always endothermic)}$$\n  </li>\n  <li>Thus, decomposition reactions are exact chemical and thermodynamic opposites of combination reactions.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>2. Three Modes of Decomposition Reactions with Balanced Equations:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Thermal Decomposition (Energy supplied as Heat $\\Delta$):</strong><br>\n    Heating green crystals of ferrous sulphate breaks it down into ferric oxide, sulphur dioxide, and sulphur trioxide:\n    $$2\\text{FeSO}_4(s) \\xrightarrow{\\Delta} \\text{Fe}_2\\text{O}_3(s) + \\text{SO}_2(g) + \\text{SO}_3(g)$$\n    <em>Opposite combination example:</em> $2\\text{Mg}(s) + \\text{O}_2(g) \\longrightarrow 2\\text{MgO}(s)$.\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Electrolytic Decomposition (Energy supplied as Electricity):</strong><br>\n    Passing electric current through molten sodium chloride or acidulated water:\n    $$2\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l) \\xrightarrow{\\text{Electric current}} 2\\text{H}_2(g) + \\text{O}_2(g)$$\n    <em>Opposite combination example:</em> $2\\text{H}_2(g) + \\text{O}_2(g) \\longrightarrow 2\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l)$.\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Photolytic Decomposition (Energy supplied as Sunlight $h\\nu$):</strong><br>\n    White silver chloride turns grey in sunlight due to decomposition into silver and chlorine gas:\n    $$2\\text{AgCl}(s) \\xrightarrow{\\text{Sunlight}} 2\\text{Ag}(s) + \\text{Cl}_2(g)$$\n    <em>Opposite combination example:</em> $2\\text{Ag}(s) + \\text{Cl}_2(g) \\longrightarrow 2\\text{AgCl}(s)$.\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Defining and contrasting combination and decomposition reactions</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Thermal decomposition definition, balanced equation, and state symbols</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Electrolytic decomposition definition, balanced equation, and state symbols</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Photolytic decomposition definition, balanced equation (black-and-white photography)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch1-q8\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch1-q8')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 8</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">In the reaction: $\\text{CuO} + \\text{H}_2 \\xrightarrow{\\Delta} \\text{Cu} + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}$, identify the substance oxidised, substance reduced, oxidising agent and reducing agent.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Concept of Redox (Reduction-Oxidation) Reactions:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Oxidation:</strong> Gain of oxygen or loss of hydrogen (or loss of electrons).</li>\n  <li><strong>Reduction:</strong> Loss of oxygen or gain of hydrogen (or gain of electrons).</li>\n  <li><strong>Oxidising Agent:</strong> The substance that gives oxygen or removes hydrogen (gets reduced).</li>\n  <li><strong>Reducing Agent:</strong> The substance that removes oxygen or adds hydrogen (gets oxidised).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>Analysis of the Reaction:</strong></p>\n$$\\text{CuO}(s) + \\text{H}_2(g) \\xrightarrow{\\Delta} \\text{Cu}(s) + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l)$$\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Substance Oxidised:</strong> $\\text{H}_2$ gains oxygen to form $\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$. Therefore, <strong>Hydrogen ($\\text{H}_2$)</strong> is oxidised.</li>\n  <li><strong>Substance Reduced:</strong> $\\text{CuO}$ loses oxygen to form metallic $\\text{Cu}$. Therefore, <strong>Copper(II) oxide ($\\text{CuO}$)</strong> is reduced.</li>\n  <li><strong>Oxidising Agent:</strong> $\\text{CuO}$ provides the oxygen and causes oxidation of $\\text{H}_2$. Hence, $\\text{CuO}$ is the oxidising agent.</li>\n  <li><strong>Reducing Agent:</strong> $\\text{H}_2$ abstracts oxygen from $\\text{CuO}$. Hence, $\\text{H}_2$ is the reducing agent.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Definitions of oxidation, reduction, oxidising and reducing agents</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Identification of oxidised substance (H2) and reduced substance (CuO)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Identification of oxidising agent (CuO) and reducing agent (H2)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case Study Questions (HOTS) — Chapter 1</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #34d399;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Case Study Question • Rancidity & Food Preservation</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\">A food technologist observed that potato chips fried in edible vegetable oil develop an unpleasant foul smell and rancid taste when left in open containers for 3 weeks, whereas chips sealed in commercial bags remain crisp and fresh for 6 months. (i) Name the chemical phenomenon responsible for spoilage. (ii) Which gas is flushed into chips packets to prevent this change and why? (iii) Name two antioxidant additives used in fatty food preservation.</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(i) Chemical Phenomenon:</strong> <strong>Rancidity</strong> (specifically oxidative rancidity). Fats and oils in open air undergo slow oxidation by atmospheric oxygen to form volatile carboxylic acids, aldehydes, and ketones with rancid odor.</li>\n  <li><strong>(ii) Gas Used:</strong> <strong>Nitrogen gas ($\\text{N}_2$)</strong>. Nitrogen is an unreactive, inert gas at room temperature due to its strong triple covalent bond ($N \\equiv N$). Flushing with nitrogen expels atmospheric oxygen and provides an inert atmosphere that prevents oxidation of fats.</li>\n  <li><strong>(iii) Antioxidant Additives:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>BHA:</strong> Butylated Hydroxyanisole</li>\n      <li><strong>BHT:</strong> Butylated Hydroxytoluene</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <div></div>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(2)\">Next Chapter →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "2": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch2\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">2</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">Science • Class 10 Board Exam (NCERT Rationalized)</div>\n      <h2>Chapter 2: Acids, Bases and Salts</h2>\n      <p>Properties, Neutralisation, pH Scale Significance in Daily Life, Preparation & Uses of Industrial Salts — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text Questions (Page 18, 22, 28)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch2-q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch2-q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why should curd and sour substances not be kept in brass and copper vessels?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Chemical Explanation and Balanced Equations:</strong></p>\n<p>When curd, lemon juice, or other sour substances (which contain organic acids like lactic acid, citric acid) are kept in brass and copper vessels, the acids react with the active copper and zinc metals present in the alloy to produce <strong>toxic, poisonous metal salts and hydrogen gas</strong>:</p>\n$$\\text{Acid} + \\text{Metal} \\longrightarrow \\text{Toxic Metal Salt} + \\text{H}_2(g)\\uparrow$$\n<p>These metal compounds make the food poisonous and unfit for human consumption, leading to severe food poisoning and gastrointestinal distress. Hence, curd and sour foodstuffs must never be stored in brass and copper containers; instead, glass, stainless steel, or tin-coated (kalai) containers are recommended.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Explaining that sour substances contain organic acids</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Stating that acids react with copper/brass forming toxic soluble metal salts causing poisoning</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch2-q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch2-q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why does an aqueous solution of an acid conduct electricity, whereas solutions of compounds like alcohol and glucose do not?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Mechanism of Electrical Conduction:</strong></p>\n<p>Electric current in solutions is carried by freely moving mobile ions.</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Aqueous Acid Solution ($HCl, H_2SO_4$):</strong><br>\n    When an acid like hydrochloric acid dissolves in water, it undergoes complete ionisation into hydronium ions ($\\text{H}_3\\text{O}^+$) and chloride ions ($\\text{Cl}^-$):\n    $$\\text{HCl}(g) + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l) \\longrightarrow \\text{H}_3\\text{O}^+(aq) + \\text{Cl}^-(aq)$$\n    The presence of these mobile ions enables the aqueous acid solution to conduct electricity.\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Glucose ($\\text{C}_6\\text{H}_{12}\\text{O}_6$) and Alcohol ($\\text{C}_2\\text{H}_5\\text{OH}$):</strong><br>\n    Although glucose and alcohol molecules contain hydrogen atoms, their hydrogen atoms are bonded by strong covalent bonds. When dissolved in water, they <strong>do not dissociate</strong> to form hydrogen ($\\text{H}^+$) or hydronium ($\\text{H}_3\\text{O}^+$) ions.\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Conclusion:</strong> In the absence of free mobile ions, solutions of glucose and alcohol do not show acidic character and do not conduct electricity.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Explaining ionisation of acids producing H3O+ ions that conduct electricity</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Explaining covalent nature of glucose/alcohol and absence of free ions</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch2-q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch2-q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why is it recommended that acid should be added to water and not water to the acid?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Safety Protocol and Thermodynamic Principle:</strong></p>\n<p>The dilution of a concentrated acid (especially concentrated sulphuric acid $\\text{H}_2\\text{SO}_4$) is an <strong>extremely exothermic process</strong> that releases a large amount of heat energy.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Why Acid must be added to Water:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>If Water is added to Concentrated Acid:</strong><br>\n    The initial drops of water react with an enormous excess of acid. The huge localized heat generated instantly boils the water into steam, causing the corrosive hot acid to <strong>splash violently out of the container</strong> onto the face, hands, or clothes of the operator, causing severe chemical burns. Furthermore, the localized thermal shock may crack and break the glass beaker.\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Safe Method (Acid added slowly to Water):</strong><br>\n    When concentrated acid is added dropwise down the side of the beaker into a large volume of water with constant stirring, the water has a very high specific heat capacity. It absorbs and dissipates the released heat evenly throughout the mixture, preventing boiling and acid splashing.\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Stating that dilution is a highly exothermic process</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Explaining catastrophic risk of acid splashing and glass shattering if water is added to acid</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Stating correct protocol: acid added slowly to water with continuous stirring</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch2-q4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch2-q4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Tooth enamel is one of the hardest substances in our body. How does it undergo damage due to eating sweets? What should be done to prevent it?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Biochemical Mechanism of Tooth Decay:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Normal Enamel:</strong> Tooth enamel is composed of <strong>calcium hydroxyapatite</strong> (a crystalline form of calcium phosphate $\\text{Ca}_3(\\text{PO}_4)_2$), which is the hardest substance in the human body. It is insoluble in water.</li>\n  <li><strong>Acid Attack (Threshold $\\text{pH} < 5.5$):</strong><br>\n    When we consume sweet, sugary, or starchy foods, bacteria present in the mouth degrade the residual carbohydrates and produce organic acids (like lactic acid). When the oral cavity pH falls <strong>below 5.5</strong>, the acid corrodes and dissolves the calcium phosphate enamel:\n    $$\\text{Enamel corrodes at pH } < 5.5$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Prevention:</strong><br>\n    Cleaning the mouth with <strong>toothpaste</strong> after meals prevents decay because toothpastes are generally <strong>basic/alkaline</strong> in nature ($\\text{pH} \\approx 8-9$). They neutralize the excess acids produced by bacteria and protect enamel from corrosion.\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Identifying enamel as calcium phosphate/hydroxyapatite and decay threshold pH < 5.5</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Explaining bacterial fermentation of sugars producing acids that corrode enamel</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Explaining neutralization role of basic toothpastes</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch2-q5\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch2-q5')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Plaster of Paris should be stored in a moisture-proof container. Explain why. Write the chemical equation for its reaction with water.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Chemical Properties and Storage of Plaster of Paris (POP):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Formula of POP:</strong> Calcium sulphate hemihydrate, $\\text{CaSO}_4 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$.</li>\n  <li><strong>Reason for Moisture-proof Storage:</strong><br>\n    Plaster of Paris is a hygroscopic white powder. When exposed to even traces of atmospheric moisture or water, it rapidly hydrates and sets into a hard, solid mass called <strong>Gypsum</strong>, losing its pliable plastering properties:\n    $$\\text{CaSO}_4 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(s) + 1\\frac{1}{2}\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l) \\longrightarrow \\text{CaSO}_4 \\cdot 2\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(s) \\quad \\text{(Gypsum - Hard solid mass)}$$\n  </li>\n  <li>Therefore, to prevent premature setting and spoilage, Plaster of Paris must strictly be stored in air-tight, moisture-proof containers.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Giving chemical formula of POP as CaSO4 . 1/2 H2O</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Writing hydration reaction forming hard gypsum CaSO4 . 2H2O</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch2-q6\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch2-q6')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 6</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Give the chemical name, chemical formula, method of preparation and two important uses of: (i) Bleaching powder, (ii) Baking soda, (iii) Washing soda.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[5 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Industrial Salts: Preparation, Chemical Formulae & Key Applications</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>1. Bleaching Powder:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Chemical Name & Formula:</strong> Calcium oxychloride, $\\text{CaOCl}_2$.</li>\n  <li><strong>Preparation:</strong> Prepared by the action of dry chlorine gas on dry slaked lime ($\\text{Ca(OH)}_2$):\n    $$\\text{Ca(OH)}_2(s) + \\text{Cl}_2(g) \\longrightarrow \\text{CaOCl}_2(s) + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l)$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Key Uses:</strong>\n    <br>(a) For bleaching cotton and linen in textile industry and wood pulp in paper factories.\n    <br>(b) As a disinfectant for sterilizing drinking water to make it germ-free.\n    <br>(c) As an oxidising agent in chemical laboratories.\n  </li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>2. Baking Soda:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Chemical Name & Formula:</strong> Sodium hydrogen carbonate (Sodium bicarbonate), $\\text{NaHCO}_3$.</li>\n  <li><strong>Preparation (Solvay Process):</strong>\n    $$\\text{NaCl} + \\text{H}_2\\text{O} + \\text{CO}_2 + \\text{NH}_3 \\longrightarrow \\text{NH}_4\\text{Cl} + \\text{NaHCO}_3\\downarrow$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Key Uses:</strong>\n    <br>(a) As an ingredient in baking powder (mixture of $\\text{NaHCO}_3$ and mild edible tartaric acid) to make cakes and bread soft and spongy by releasing $\\text{CO}_2$:\n    $$\\text{NaHCO}_3 + \\text{H}^+ \\longrightarrow \\text{CO}_2\\uparrow + \\text{H}_2\\text{O} + \\text{Sodium salt of acid}$$\n    <br>(b) As an active antacid to neutralize excess stomach acidity.\n    <br>(c) In soda-acid fire extinguishers.\n  </li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>3. Washing Soda:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Chemical Name & Formula:</strong> Sodium carbonate decahydrate, $\\text{Na}_2\\text{CO}_3 \\cdot 10\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$.</li>\n  <li><strong>Preparation:</strong> Recrystallisation of anhydrous sodium carbonate (soda ash) from water:\n    $$\\text{Na}_2\\text{CO}_3(s) + 10\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l) \\longrightarrow \\text{Na}_2\\text{CO}_3 \\cdot 10\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(s)$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Key Uses:</strong>\n    <br>(a) In glass, soap, and paper industries.\n    <br>(b) For removing permanent hardness of water (precipitating $\\text{Ca}^{2+}$ and $\\text{Mg}^{2+}$ ions).\n    <br>(c) As a domestic cleansing agent for laundry.\n  </li>\n</ul>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Bleaching powder: Formula, preparation equation from Ca(OH)2 + Cl2, and 2 uses</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Baking soda: Formula, Solvay equation, role in baking powder/antacids</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Washing soda: Formula, recrystallisation equation, and 2 major industrial uses</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case Study Questions (HOTS) — Chapter 2</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #34d399;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Case Study Question • pH in Human Digestion & Antacids</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\">A patient visited a doctor complaining of acute burning sensation and sharp pain in the upper abdomen after having a spicy meal. The doctor diagnosed hyperacidity and prescribed a milk of magnesia suspension. (i) Name the acid produced naturally in our stomach and state its biological role. (ii) What is milk of magnesia? Write its chemical formula. (iii) Write the neutralization reaction that takes place in the patient's stomach.</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(i) Natural Stomach Acid:</strong> <strong>Hydrochloric acid ($\\text{HCl}$)</strong>. It maintains a strongly acidic pH ($\\approx 1.2 - 2.0$) necessary to activate the gastric enzyme propepsin into active pepsin for protein digestion, and kills harmful bacteria ingested with food.</li>\n  <li><strong>(ii) Milk of Magnesia:</strong> It is a mild, non-corrosive suspension of <strong>Magnesium hydroxide</strong> in water. <strong>Chemical Formula:</strong> $\\text{Mg(OH)}_2$.</li>\n  <li><strong>(iii) Neutralization Reaction:</strong><br>\n    $$\\text{Mg(OH)}_2(aq) + 2\\text{HCl}(aq) \\longrightarrow \\text{MgCl}_2(aq) + 2\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l)$$\n    It safely neutralizes the excess stomach acid without damaging the delicate mucous membrane lining.\n  </li>\n</ol></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(1)\">← Previous Chapter</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(3)\">Next Chapter →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "3": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch3\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">3</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">Science • Class 10 Board Exam (NCERT Rationalized)</div>\n      <h2>Chapter 3: Metals and Non-metals</h2>\n      <p>Physical & Chemical Properties, Amphoteric Oxides, Ionic Compounds & Extraction Metallurgy — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text Questions (Page 40, 46, 49, 53)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch3-q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch3-q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Give an example of a metal which: (i) is a liquid at room temperature, (ii) can be easily cut with a knife, (iii) is the best conductor of heat, (iv) is a poor conductor of heat.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Physical Exceptions in Metals and Non-metals:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(i) Liquid metal at room temperature:</strong> <strong>Mercury ($\\text{Hg}$)</strong>. All other metals are crystalline solids.</li>\n  <li><strong>(ii) Metal easily cut with a knife:</strong> <strong>Sodium ($\\text{Na}$)</strong> and <strong>Potassium ($\\text{K}$)</strong>. They are soft alkali metals with low density and weak metallic bonding.</li>\n  <li><strong>(iii) Best conductor of heat:</strong> <strong>Silver ($\\text{Ag}$)</strong>, closely followed by Copper ($\\text{Cu}$).</li>\n  <li><strong>(iv) Poorest conductor of heat:</strong> <strong>Lead ($\\text{Pb}$)</strong> and <strong>Mercury ($\\text{Hg}$)</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Additional Key Exceptions:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Lustrous non-metal:</em> <strong>Iodine ($\\text{I}_2$)</strong>.</li>\n      <li><em>Liquid non-metal at room temperature:</em> <strong>Bromine ($\\text{Br}_2$)</strong>.</li>\n      <li><em>Non-metal conducting electricity:</em> <strong>Graphite</strong> (allotrope of carbon).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Correct identification of liquid metal (Mercury) and soft metal (Sodium/Potassium)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Correct identification of best conductor (Silver) and poor conductor (Lead)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch3-q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch3-q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What are amphoteric oxides? Give two examples of amphoteric oxides. Write chemical equations showing amphoteric nature of aluminium oxide.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Definition:</strong></p>\n<p>Metal oxides that show both <strong>acidic as well as basic behaviour</strong> by reacting with both acids and bases to produce salt and water are called <strong>Amphoteric Oxides</strong>.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Two Examples:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Aluminium oxide ($\\text{Al}_2\\text{O}_3$)</strong></li>\n  <li><strong>Zinc oxide ($\\text{ZnO}$)</strong></li>\n</ol>\n\n<p><strong>Balanced Chemical Equations for Aluminium Oxide ($\\text{Al}_2\\text{O}_3$):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(a) Basic behaviour (Reaction with Acid $\\text{HCl}$):</strong><br>\n    $$\\text{Al}_2\\text{O}_3(s) + 6\\text{HCl}(aq) \\longrightarrow 2\\text{AlCl}_3(aq) + 3\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l)$$\n    Here, aluminium oxide acts as a base and reacts with acid to yield aluminium chloride (salt) and water.\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(b) Acidic behaviour (Reaction with Strong Base $\\text{NaOH}$):</strong><br>\n    $$\\text{Al}_2\\text{O}_3(s) + 2\\text{NaOH}(aq) \\longrightarrow 2\\text{NaAlO}_2(aq) + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l)$$\n    $$\\text{Aluminium oxide} + \\text{Sodium hydroxide} \\longrightarrow \\text{Sodium aluminate} + \\text{Water}$$\n    Here, aluminium oxide acts as an acidic oxide, reacting with the base to form the soluble complex salt sodium aluminate.\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Definition of amphoteric oxides and naming Al2O3 and ZnO</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Balanced equation with acid (HCl) yielding AlCl3</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Balanced equation with base (NaOH) yielding sodium aluminate NaAlO2</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch3-q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch3-q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why is hydrogen gas not evolved when a metal reacts with nitric acid? Name two metals which react with very dilute nitric acid to evolve hydrogen gas.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Chemical Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>When most metals react with dilute mineral acids, hydrogen gas is normally evolved. However, when a metal reacts with <strong>Nitric acid ($\\text{HNO}_3$)</strong>, hydrogen gas ($\\text{H}_2$) is <strong>not evolved</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Reason:</strong> Nitric acid is a <strong>strong oxidising agent</strong>. As soon as hydrogen gas ($\\text{H}_2$) is liberated at the metal surface, $\\text{HNO}_3$ rapidly oxidises the nascent hydrogen into <strong>water ($\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$)</strong> and itself gets reduced to any of the nitrogen oxides ($\\text{NO}_2, \\text{NO}, \\text{or } \\text{N}_2\\text{O}$).</li>\n  <li><strong>Exceptions:</strong> Very dilute nitric acid (about $1\\% \\text{ HNO}_3$) reacts with <strong>Magnesium ($\\text{Mg}$)</strong> and <strong>Manganese ($\\text{Mn}$)</strong> to evolve hydrogen gas:\n    $$\\text{Mg}(s) + 2\\text{HNO}_3(\\text{very dil}) \\longrightarrow \\text{Mg(NO}_3)_2(aq) + \\text{H}_2(g)\\uparrow$$\n    $$\\text{Mn}(s) + 2\\text{HNO}_3(\\text{very dil}) \\longrightarrow \\text{Mn(NO}_3)_2(aq) + \\text{H}_2(g)\\uparrow$$\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Explaining HNO3 is a strong oxidising agent that oxidises H2 to H2O</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Naming exceptions Magnesium and Manganese with very dilute HNO3</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch3-q4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch3-q4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Show the formation of magnesium chloride ($\\text{MgCl}_2$) by the transfer of electrons. Explain why ionic compounds have high melting points and conduct electricity in the molten state.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Electron Transfer and Ionic Bond Formation in $\\text{MgCl}_2$:</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>1. Electronic Configurations:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Magnesium ($Z = 12$): Electronic configuration $= 2, 8, 2$. It has 2 valence electrons. To attain stable neon octet ($2, 8$), it readily loses 2 valence electrons:\n    $$\\text{Mg} \\longrightarrow \\text{Mg}^{2+} + 2e^-$$\n  </li>\n  <li>Chlorine ($Z = 17$): Electronic configuration $= 2, 8, 7$. It has 7 valence electrons. To attain stable argon octet ($2, 8, 8$), each chlorine atom accepts 1 electron:\n    $$\\text{Cl} + e^- \\longrightarrow \\text{Cl}^-$$\n  </li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>2. Electron-Dot Representation:</strong></p>\n<p>One magnesium atom transfers one electron each to two separate chlorine atoms:</p>\n$$\\text{Mg}^{\\bullet\\bullet} + 2 \\times \\cdot\\underset{\\bullet\\bullet}{\\overset{\\bullet\\bullet}{\\text{Cl}}}: \\longrightarrow [\\text{Mg}]^{2+} \\left[:\\underset{\\bullet\\bullet}{\\overset{\\bullet\\bullet}{\\text{Cl}}}:^-\\right]_2 \\implies \\text{MgCl}_2$$\n\n<p><strong>3. Physical Properties of Ionic Compounds:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>High Melting and Boiling Points:</strong> Ionic solids consist of closely packed cations and anions held together by extremely strong electrostatic forces of attraction. A huge amount of thermal energy is required to overcome these strong inter-ionic bonds.</li>\n  <li><strong>Electrical Conductivity:</strong> In the solid state, ions are locked rigidly in fixed lattice positions and cannot move; hence solid ionic compounds are insulators. However, in <strong>molten state or aqueous solution</strong>, the crystal lattice breaks down and free mobile ions are available to conduct electricity.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Electronic configurations and ion formation Mg2+ and Cl-</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Electron-dot transfer diagram for MgCl2</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Scientific explanation for high melting point and conductivity in molten/aqueous state</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch3-q5\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch3-q5')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Differentiate between roasting and calcination with examples. Explain how zinc is extracted from its sulphide and carbonate ores with balanced equations.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[5 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Comparison: Roasting vs Calcination</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin:12px 0; font-size:0.88rem;\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"background:#f1f5f9; text-align:left;\">\n      <th style=\"padding:8px 12px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Feature</th>\n      <th style=\"padding:8px 12px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Roasting</th>\n      <th style=\"padding:8px 12px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Calcination</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px 12px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Definition</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px 12px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Heating ore strongly in the <strong>excess presence of air</strong> below its melting point.</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px 12px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Heating ore strongly in the <strong>absence or limited supply of air</strong> below its melting point.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px 12px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Type of Ore</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px 12px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Applicable primarily to <strong>Sulphide ores</strong>.</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px 12px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Applicable primarily to <strong>Carbonate and Hydrated oxide ores</strong>.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px 12px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Gas Evolved</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px 12px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Sulphur dioxide gas ($\\text{SO}_2$) is evolved.</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px 12px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Carbon dioxide gas ($\\text{CO}_2$) or moisture ($\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$) is driven off.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<p><strong>2. Balanced Chemical Reactions for Zinc Ore:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Roasting of Zinc Blende ($\\text{ZnS}$):</strong><br>\n    $$2\\text{ZnS}(s) + 3\\text{O}_2(g) \\xrightarrow{\\Delta} 2\\text{ZnO}(s) + 2\\text{SO}_2(g)\\uparrow$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Calcination of Calamine ($\\text{ZnCO}_3$):</strong><br>\n    $$\\text{ZnCO}_3(s) \\xrightarrow{\\Delta} \\text{ZnO}(s) + \\text{CO}_2(g)\\uparrow$$\n  </li>\n</ol>\n\n<p><strong>3. Reduction of Zinc Oxide to Zinc Metal:</strong></p>\n<p>The zinc oxide formed is subsequently reduced to metallic zinc by heating with carbon (coke) as a reducing agent in a retort:</p>\n$$\\text{ZnO}(s) + \\text{C}(s) \\xrightarrow{\\Delta} \\text{Zn}(g) + \\text{CO}(g)$$\n<p>Zinc vapours are distilled and condensed into solid pure zinc.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Clear tabular differentiation between roasting and calcination (3 points)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Balanced equation for roasting of ZnS</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Balanced equation for calcination of ZnCO3</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Reduction equation of ZnO with coke C -> Zn + CO</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch3-q6\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch3-q6')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 6</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Explain the process of electrolytic refining of copper with electrode reactions and state what is meant by anode mud.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Electrolytic Refining of Copper ($\\text{Cu}$):</strong></p>\n<p>Impure blister copper is refined to $99.99\\%$ purity using an electrolytic cell.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Cell Setup:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Anode (positive electrode):</strong> Thick block of <strong>impure copper</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cathode (negative electrode):</strong> Thin strip of <strong>pure copper</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Electrolyte:</strong> Acidified aqueous solution of <strong>copper sulphate ($\\text{CuSO}_4 + \\text{dil. H}_2\\text{SO}_4$)</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"sci-diagram-wrap\" style=\"margin: 18px auto; text-align: center; max-width: 480px; background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #d1fae5; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; box-shadow: 0 2px 8px rgba(5,150,105,0.06);\">\n  <svg viewBox=\"0 0 360 230\" width=\"100%\" height=\"200\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <!-- Tank -->\n  <rect x=\"50\" y=\"50\" width=\"260\" height=\"150\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#f0f9ff\" stroke=\"#0284c7\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/>\n  <!-- Electrolyte level -->\n  <rect x=\"52\" y=\"75\" width=\"256\" height=\"123\" fill=\"#bae6fd\" opacity=\"0.45\"/>\n  <!-- Impure Anode (+) -->\n  <rect x=\"90\" y=\"30\" width=\"25\" height=\"140\" fill=\"#ea580c\" stroke=\"#9a3412\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text x=\"102\" y=\"20\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#9a3412\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Anode (+)</text>\n  <text x=\"102\" y=\"105\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"9\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Impure Cu</text>\n  <!-- Pure Cathode (-) -->\n  <rect x=\"235\" y=\"30\" width=\"12\" height=\"140\" fill=\"#dc2626\" stroke=\"#991b1b\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text x=\"241\" y=\"20\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#dc2626\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Cathode (-)</text>\n  <text x=\"241\" y=\"105\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"9\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Pure Cu</text>\n  <!-- Anode mud -->\n  <path d=\"M 80 190 Q 102 182 125 190 Z\" fill=\"#475569\"/>\n  <text x=\"102\" y=\"205\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#334155\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Anode Mud (Au, Ag)</text>\n  <!-- Cu2+ ions moving -->\n  <circle cx=\"165\" cy=\"115\" r=\"14\" fill=\"#0284c7\" opacity=\"0.8\"/>\n  <text x=\"165\" y=\"119\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Cu²⁺ →</text>\n  <text x=\"180\" y=\"165\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#0369a1\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Acidified CuSO₄ Solution</text>\n</svg>\n  <div style=\"font-size: 0.8rem; font-weight: 600; color: #065f46; margin-top: 8px;\">Figure: Electrolytic Refining Tank for Copper with Anode Mud Formation</div>\n</div>\n<p><strong>Electrode Reactions:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>At Anode (Oxidation):</strong> Copper atoms from the impure anode lose electrons and dissolve into the electrolyte as cupric ions:\n    $$\\text{Cu}(s) \\longrightarrow \\text{Cu}^{2+}(aq) + 2e^-$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>At Cathode (Reduction):</strong> An equivalent amount of pure $\\text{Cu}^{2+}$ ions from the solution migrate to the cathode, gain electrons, and deposit as pure copper metal:\n    $$\\text{Cu}^{2+}(aq) + 2e^- \\longrightarrow \\text{Cu}(s)$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Anode Mud:</strong> Soluble impurities pass into the solution, whereas insoluble, less reactive noble metal impurities (such as Gold $\\text{Au}$, Silver $\\text{Ag}$, Platinum $\\text{Pt}$) settle below the anode at the bottom of the tank as <strong>anode mud</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Specifying anode, cathode, and acidified copper sulphate electrolyte</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Writing anode oxidation and cathode reduction equations</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Explaining formation of pure cathode deposit and anode mud containing Au/Ag</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case Study Questions (HOTS) — Chapter 3</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #34d399;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Case Study Question • Corrosion & Galvanisation</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\">In railway tracks, joints of broken steel rails are welded on-site using the Thermit process. Furthermore, coastal bridges made of iron are galvanised with zinc. (i) Write the balanced chemical equation for the Thermit reaction and explain why it is useful for welding rails. (ii) How does zinc prevent rusting of iron even if the zinc layer gets scratched?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(i) Thermit Reaction:</strong><br>\n    $$\\text{Fe}_2\\text{O}_3(s) + 2\\text{Al}(s) \\longrightarrow 2\\text{Fe}(l) + \\text{Al}_2\\text{O}_3(s) + \\text{Enormous Heat}$$\n    Aluminium is more reactive than iron and displaces it from ferric oxide. The reaction is so highly exothermic that the iron is produced in the <strong>molten liquid state</strong>. This molten iron flows directly into the gap between broken rail joints, welding them solidly upon solidification.\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(ii) Galvanisation & Sacrificial Protection:</strong><br>\n    Zinc is more electropositive (more reactive) than iron. When the surface layer is scratched, zinc acts as a <strong>sacrificial anode</strong>. Zinc corrodes preferentially by losing electrons ($\\text{Zn} \\longrightarrow \\text{Zn}^{2+} + 2e^-$), supplying electrons to the iron and preventing iron from oxidising to rust.\n  </li>\n</ol></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(2)\">← Previous Chapter</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(4)\">Next Chapter →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "4": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch4\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">4</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">Science • Class 10 Board Exam (NCERT Rationalized)</div>\n      <h2>Chapter 4: Carbon and its Compounds</h2>\n      <p>Covalent Bonding, Versatile Nature of Carbon, Homologous Series, Functional Groups, Ethanol, Ethanoic Acid & Soaps — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text Questions (Page 61, 68, 71, 74)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch4-q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch4-q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What are the two properties of carbon which lead to the huge number of carbon compounds we see around us?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Two Unique Properties of Carbon:</strong></p>\n<p>Carbon forms an exceptionally large number of compounds (over 3 million organic compounds) due to two unique chemical properties:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>1. Catenation:</strong><br>\n    Catenation is the unique ability of carbon atoms to form strong covalent bonds with other carbon atoms to give rise to long open chains, branched chains, or closed ring structures:\n    <ul>\n      <li>Carbon can form single bonds ($\\text{C}-\\text{C}$), double bonds ($\\text{C}=\\text{C}$), and triple bonds ($\\text{C}\\equiv\\text{C}$).</li>\n      <li>Carbon-carbon ($\\text{C}-\\text{C}$) bonds are exceptionally strong and stable (bond dissociation energy $\\approx 348\\text{ kJ/mol}$) due to the small atomic size of carbon, which enables the nucleus to hold the shared electron pairs firmly. (Silicon shows catenation up to 7-8 atoms, but those chains are highly unstable and reactive).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>2. Tetravalency:</strong><br>\n    Carbon has an atomic number of 6 with an electronic configuration of $2, 4$. Possessing 4 valence electrons, it has a valency of 4. It can share electrons with four other carbon atoms or with atoms of other monovalent elements like Hydrogen ($\\text{H}$), Chlorine ($\\text{Cl}$), Oxygen ($\\text{O}$), Nitrogen ($\\text{N}$), and Sulphur ($\\text{S}$), yielding a vast spectrum of compounds with diverse functional properties.\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Detailed explanation of Catenation with bond stability due to small size</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Detailed explanation of Tetravalency and forming covalent bonds with various elements</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch4-q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch4-q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What is a homologous series? State any three characteristics of members of a homologous series.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Definition and Characteristics of a Homologous Series:</strong></p>\n<p>A <strong>Homologous Series</strong> is a group or family of structurally similar carbon compounds in which members possess the <strong>same functional group</strong>, exhibit similar chemical properties, and consecutive members differ by a fixed molecular unit.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Key Characteristics:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Successive Difference:</strong> Any two consecutive members of a homologous series differ in their molecular formula by a <strong>$-\\text{CH}_2-$ (methylene) group</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Molecular Mass Difference:</strong> The molecular mass of any two adjacent members differs by exactly <strong>$14\\text{ u}$</strong> (Carbon = $12\\text{ u}$, $2 \\times \\text{Hydrogen} = 2\\text{ u}$).</li>\n  <li><strong>Same General Formula:</strong> All members share a common general formula (e.g. Alkanes: $\\text{C}_n\\text{H}_{2n+2}$, Alcohols: $\\text{C}_n\\text{H}_{2n+1}\\text{OH}$).</li>\n  <li><strong>Gradation in Physical Properties:</strong> Melting points, boiling points, and densities show a steady, regular increase with increasing molecular mass due to increasing van der Waals forces.</li>\n  <li><strong>Identical Chemical Properties:</strong> Because all members have the same functional group, their chemical reactions and chemical reactivity are essentially identical.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Precise definition of homologous series</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Difference by -CH2- unit and 14 u mass difference</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Gradation in physical properties and identical chemical properties</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch4-q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch4-q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How many structural isomers can you draw for pentane? Draw their structures and write their IUPAC names.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Structural Isomers of Pentane ($\\text{C}_5\\text{H}_{12}$):</strong></p>\n<p>Compounds having the <strong>same molecular formula</strong> but <strong>different structural arrangements</strong> of atoms are called structural isomers. Pentane has <strong>3 structural isomers</strong>:</p>\n\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>1. n-Pentane (Pentane):</strong><br>\n    Unbranched straight carbon chain:<br>\n    $$\\text{CH}_3-\\text{CH}_2-\\text{CH}_2-\\text{CH}_2-\\text{CH}_3$$\n    <em>IUPAC Name:</em> Pentane.\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>2. Isopentane (2-Methylbutane):</strong><br>\n    Four-carbon parent chain with a methyl branch at carbon-2:<br>\n    $$\\text{CH}_3-\\text{CH}(\\text{CH}_3)-\\text{CH}_2-\\text{CH}_3$$\n    <em>IUPAC Name:</em> 2-Methylbutane.\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>3. Neopentane (2,2-Dimethylpropane):</strong><br>\n    Three-carbon parent chain with two methyl branches at carbon-2:<br>\n    $$\\text{C}(\\text{CH}_3)_4$$\n    <em>IUPAC Name:</em> 2,2-Dimethylpropane.\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Defining structural isomers</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Formula and IUPAC name of n-pentane and 2-methylbutane</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Formula and IUPAC name of 2,2-dimethylpropane</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch4-q4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch4-q4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How would you distinguish experimentally between an alcohol and a carboxylic acid?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Distinguishing Ethanol ($\\text{C}_2\\text{H}_5\\text{OH}$) and Ethanoic Acid ($\\text{CH}_3\\text{COOH}$):</strong></p>\n\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Test 1: Sodium Hydrogen Carbonate Test ($\\text{NaHCO}_3$ / Baking Soda):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Ethanoic Acid:</strong> Reacts briskly with sodium hydrogen carbonate to produce <strong>brisk effervescence of colourless, odourless Carbon dioxide gas ($\\text{CO}_2$)</strong>, which turns lime water milky:\n        $$\\text{CH}_3\\text{COOH}(l) + \\text{NaHCO}_3(s) \\longrightarrow \\text{CH}_3\\text{COONa}(aq) + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l) + \\text{CO}_2(g)\\uparrow$$\n      </li>\n      <li><strong>Ethanol:</strong> Being an extremely weak neutral compound, ethanol <strong>does not react</strong> with sodium hydrogen carbonate; no effervescence is observed.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Test 2: Litmus Paper Test:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Ethanoic Acid:</strong> Turns blue litmus paper <strong>red</strong> due to liberation of $\\text{H}^+$ ions.</li>\n      <li><strong>Ethanol:</strong> Neutral liquid; has no effect on either blue or red litmus paper.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Sodium hydrogen carbonate test with chemical reaction and brisk effervescence for ethanoic acid</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Litmus paper test distinction: blue litmus turns red with ethanoic acid, neutral with ethanol</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch4-q5\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch4-q5')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Explain the terms: (i) Esterification, (ii) Saponification. Write chemical equations for both reactions and state their practical applications.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[5 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>1. Esterification Reaction:</strong></p>\n<p>When an alcohol reacts with a carboxylic acid in the presence of a few drops of concentrated sulphuric acid ($\\text{H}_2\\text{SO}_4$) as a dehydrating catalyst, a sweet, fruity-smelling compound called an <strong>Ester</strong> is formed:</p>\n$$\\text{CH}_3\\text{COOH}(l) + \\text{C}_2\\text{H}_5\\text{OH}(l) \\xrightarrow{\\text{Conc. H}_2\\text{SO}_4, \\Delta} \\text{CH}_3\\text{COOC}_2\\text{H}_5(l) + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l)$$\n$$\\text{(Ethanoic acid)} + \\text{(Ethanol)} \\longrightarrow \\text{(Ethyl ethanoate - Ester)} + \\text{(Water)}$$\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Characteristics:</strong> Esters have pleasant, fruity smells and are widely used in making perfumes, artificial flavouring essences, and cosmetics.</li>\n  <li><strong>Role of Conc. $\\text{H}_2\\text{SO}_4$:</strong> It acts as an acid catalyst and a dehydrating agent that absorbs water, shifting the reversible equilibrium forward to maximize ester yield.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>2. Saponification Reaction (Preparation of Soap):</strong></p>\n<p>When an ester is heated with an aqueous alkali (strong base like sodium hydroxide $\\text{NaOH}$), the ester is hydrolyzed back into sodium salt of carboxylic acid and alcohol:</p>\n$$\\text{CH}_3\\text{COOC}_2\\text{H}_5(l) + \\text{NaOH}(aq) \\xrightarrow{\\Delta} \\text{CH}_3\\text{COONa}(aq) + \\text{C}_2\\text{H}_5\\text{OH}(l)$$\n$$\\text{(Ethyl ethanoate)} + \\text{(Sodium hydroxide)} \\longrightarrow \\text{(Sodium ethanoate)} + \\text{(Ethanol)}$$\n<ul>\n  <li>Since this reaction is used in the commercial preparation of soap by hydrolyzing higher fatty acid triglycerides (vegetable oils or animal fats) with $\\text{NaOH}$, it is called <strong>Saponification</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Esterification definition, catalyst conc H2SO4, and balanced equation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Saponification definition and balanced equation with NaOH</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Uses of esters in perfumes/flavouring and soaps formation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch4-q6\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch4-q6')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 6</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Explain the mechanism of the cleansing action of soaps. What is a micelle? Why does soap not form lather with hard water?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[4 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Structure and Cleansing Action of Soap Molecules:</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>1. Molecular Structure of Soap:</strong></p>\n<p>A soap molecule is a sodium or potassium salt of long-chain fatty acids (e.g. sodium stearate $\\text{C}_{17}\\text{H}_{35}\\text{COO}^-\\text{Na}^+$). It has two distinct parts:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Hydrophobic Tail:</strong> A long non-polar hydrocarbon chain ($\\text{C}_{17}\\text{H}_{35}-$). It is water-repelling (hydrophobic) and oil-soluble.</li>\n  <li><strong>Hydrophilic Head:</strong> A short ionic polar carboxylate group ($-\\text{COO}^-\\text{Na}^+$). It is water-loving (hydrophilic) and soluble in water.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p><strong>2. Mechanism of Micelle Formation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Most dirt and grease on dirty clothes is oily and hydrophobic in nature.</li>\n  <li>When soap is added to water, the hydrophobic hydrocarbon tails point inwards into the oily dirt droplet, dissolving in the oil.</li>\n  <li>The hydrophilic ionic heads point outwards toward the surrounding water.</li>\n  <li>This spherical aggregate of soap molecules trapping oil at its core is called a <strong>Micelle</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"sci-diagram-wrap\" style=\"margin: 18px auto; text-align: center; max-width: 480px; background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #d1fae5; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; box-shadow: 0 2px 8px rgba(5,150,105,0.06);\">\n  <svg viewBox=\"0 0 320 230\" width=\"100%\" height=\"200\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <!-- Central oil droplet -->\n  <circle cx=\"160\" cy=\"115\" r=\"35\" fill=\"#fde047\" stroke=\"#ca8a04\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"160\" y=\"119\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#854d0e\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Oily Dirt</text>\n  <!-- Surrounding soap molecules -->\n  <!-- Radiating tails and heads -->\n  <g stroke=\"#0284c7\" stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"none\">\n    <path d=\"M 160 80 Q 155 65 160 50\"/>\n    <path d=\"M 160 150 Q 165 165 160 180\"/>\n    <path d=\"M 125 115 Q 110 110 95 115\"/>\n    <path d=\"M 195 115 Q 210 120 225 115\"/>\n    <path d=\"M 135 90 Q 120 80 110 70\"/>\n    <path d=\"M 185 90 Q 200 80 210 70\"/>\n    <path d=\"M 135 140 Q 120 150 110 160\"/>\n    <path d=\"M 185 140 Q 200 150 210 160\"/>\n  </g>\n  <!-- Hydrophilic ionic heads (COO- Na+) -->\n  <g fill=\"#dc2626\">\n    <circle cx=\"160\" cy=\"46\" r=\"5\"/><circle cx=\"160\" cy=\"184\" r=\"5\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"91\" cy=\"115\" r=\"5\"/><circle cx=\"229\" cy=\"115\" r=\"5\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"107\" cy=\"66\" r=\"5\"/><circle cx=\"213\" cy=\"66\" r=\"5\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"107\" cy=\"164\" r=\"5\"/><circle cx=\"213\" cy=\"164\" r=\"5\"/>\n  </g>\n  <text x=\"235\" y=\"45\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#dc2626\">Hydrophilic Head (-COO⁻)</text>\n  <text x=\"45\" y=\"195\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#0284c7\">Hydrophobic Tail</text>\n</svg>\n  <div style=\"font-size: 0.8rem; font-weight: 600; color: #065f46; margin-top: 8px;\">Figure: Soap Micelle Trapping Oily Dirt in Center with Hydrophilic Heads Outwards</div>\n</div>\n<p><strong>3. Emulsion and Rinsing:</strong></p>\n<p>The negatively charged heads on the surface of micelles repel one another, preventing the oily globules from coalescing into larger droplets. When the cloth is agitated mechanically and rinsed with water, the micelles carrying the trapped oily dirt are washed away, leaving the fabric clean.</p>\n\n<p><strong>4. Why Soap is Ineffective in Hard Water:</strong></p>\n<p>Hard water contains dissolved calcium ($\\text{Ca}^{2+}$) and magnesium ($\\text{Mg}^{2+}$) ions. These ions react with soap molecules to form an insoluble greyish curdy precipitate called <strong>Scum</strong>:</p>\n$$2\\text{C}_{17}\\text{H}_{35}\\text{COONa} + \\text{Ca}^{2+} \\longrightarrow (\\text{C}_{17}\\text{H}_{35}\\text{COO})_2\\text{Ca}\\downarrow + 2\\text{Na}^+$$\n<p>This scum sticks to cloth fibers and wastes soap. Synthetic detergents do not form scum because their sulphonate/sulphate salts with calcium and magnesium are water-soluble.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Structure of soap: hydrophobic hydrocarbon tail and hydrophilic ionic head</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Mechanism of micelle formation and emulsification of oily dirt</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Explanation of scum formation in hard water due to Ca2+/Mg2+ ions</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case Study Questions (HOTS) — Chapter 4</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #34d399;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Case Study Question • Ethanol Consumption & Denatured Alcohol</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\">Pure ethanol is used widely in chemical industries and pharmaceuticals. However, commercial industrial alcohol is deliberately converted into 'denatured alcohol'. Furthermore, alcohol consumption affects human health. (i) What is denatured alcohol and how is it prepared? (ii) What happens biologically when a person drinks adulterated alcohol containing methanol? (iii) Write the reaction of ethanol with sodium metal.</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(i) Denatured Alcohol:</strong> Industrial ethanol is made unfit for drinking to prevent its misuse and tax evasion by adding poisonous substances such as <strong>Methanol ($\\text{CH}_3\\text{OH}$)</strong>, pyridine, and copper sulphate dye (giving it a blue colour). This poisoned alcohol is called denatured alcohol.</li>\n  <li><strong>(ii) Biological Effect of Methanol:</strong> Methanol is oxidised in the human liver by alcohol dehydrogenase to <strong>methanal (formaldehyde)</strong>. Methanal reacts rapidly with protoplasm, causing cellular coagulation similar to an egg curdling. It also damages the optic nerve, causing permanent <strong>blindness</strong> and respiratory arrest leading to death.</li>\n  <li><strong>(iii) Reaction with Sodium:</strong><br>\n    $$2\\text{C}_2\\text{H}_5\\text{OH}(l) + 2\\text{Na}(s) \\longrightarrow 2\\text{C}_2\\text{H}_5\\text{ONa}(alc) + \\text{H}_2(g)\\uparrow$$\n    It forms sodium ethoxide with brisk effervescence of hydrogen gas.\n  </li>\n</ol></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(3)\">← Previous Chapter</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(5)\">Next Chapter →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "5": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch5\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">5</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">Science • Class 10 Board Exam (NCERT Rationalized)</div>\n      <h2>Chapter 5: Life Processes</h2>\n      <p>Nutrition (Photosynthesis & Digestion), Respiration, Transportation (Heart & Double Circulation) & Excretion (Nephron) — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text Questions (Page 83, 89, 96, 99)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch5-q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch5-q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What are the major events that occur during the process of photosynthesis? Write the balanced chemical equation.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Major Biochemical Events during Photosynthesis:</strong></p>\n<p>Photosynthesis is the autotrophic process by which green plants synthesize carbohydrates (glucose) from inorganic carbon dioxide and water in the presence of sunlight and chlorophyll:</p>\n$$6\\text{CO}_2(aq) + 12\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l) \\xrightarrow[\\text{Chlorophyll}]{\\text{Sunlight}} \\text{C}_6\\text{H}_{12}\\text{O}_6(aq) + 6\\text{O}_2(g)\\uparrow + 6\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l)$$\n\n<p><strong>Three Sequential Events:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>1. Absorption of Light Energy:</strong> Chlorophyll pigments located in the thylakoid membranes of chloroplasts absorb solar radiant energy.</li>\n  <li><strong>2. Conversion of Light Energy into Chemical Energy & Photolysis of Water:</strong><br>\n    The absorbed light energy excites electrons, driving the photolysis (splitting) of water molecules into hydrogen ions, electrons, and molecular oxygen gas:\n    $$2\\text{H}_2\\text{O} \\xrightarrow{\\text{Light, Chlorophyll}} 4\\text{H}^+ + 4e^- + \\text{O}_2\\uparrow$$\n    Chemical energy is assimilated as ATP and $\\text{NADPH}$.\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>3. Reduction of Carbon Dioxide:</strong><br>\n    During the light-independent (dark) enzymatic reactions (Calvin cycle) occurring in the stroma, carbon dioxide is reduced to carbohydrates (glucose) using the assimilatory power of ATP and $\\text{NADPH}$.\n  </li>\n</ol>\n<p><em>Desert Plant Adaptation:</em> Desert plants (CAM plants) take up $\\text{CO}_2$ at night and store it as an intermediate organic acid (malic acid) to prevent daytime transpirational water loss. During the day, they use absorbed sunlight to complete carbohydrate synthesis.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Balanced photosynthesis equation with state symbols</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Three sequential steps: Absorption of light, Photolysis of water, Reduction of CO2</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Note on desert plant adaptation (stomata opening at night)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">0.5 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch5-q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch5-q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How are fats digested in our bodies? Where does this process take place? Explain the role of bile juice and pancreatic lipase.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Enzymatic Digestion in the Human Small Intestine:</strong></p>\n<p>The small intestine is the site of complete digestion of carbohydrates, proteins, and fats. It receives secretions from two major glands:</p>\n\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>1. Secretion from Liver — Bile Juice:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Neutralization:</em> The food coming from the stomach is highly acidic ($\\text{pH} \\approx 1.5 - 2$). Bile juice is alkaline and neutralizes the acidic chyme, creating an alkaline medium ($\\text{pH} \\approx 7.8 - 8.4$) necessary for pancreatic enzymes to function.</li>\n      <li><em>Emulsification of Fats:</em> Fats are present in the intestine as large insoluble globules, which are difficult for water-soluble enzymes to break down. <strong>Bile salts</strong> break these large fat globules down into fine droplets (micelles), enormously increasing their surface area for enzyme action.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>2. Secretion from Pancreas — Pancreatic Juice:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Pancreatic Amylase:</strong> Hydrolyzes remaining starch into maltose.</li>\n      <li><strong>Trypsin:</strong> Digests proteins, peptones, and proteoses into smaller peptides.</li>\n      <li><strong>Pancreatic Lipase:</strong> Breaks down emulsified triglycerides into fatty acids and glycerol.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>3. Intestinal Juice (Succus Entericus):</strong><br>\n    Enzymes secreted by the intestinal mucosal cells complete the digestion:\n    <ul>\n      <li>Complex Carbohydrates $\\longrightarrow$ <strong>Glucose</strong></li>\n      <li>Proteins $\\longrightarrow$ <strong>Amino acids</strong></li>\n      <li>Fats $\\longrightarrow$ <strong>Fatty acids and Glycerol</strong></li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Role of bile juice: making chyme alkaline and emulsifying fats with bile salts</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Role of pancreatic enzymes: trypsin (proteins) and lipase (emulsified fats)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch5-q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch5-q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Explain the three different pathways by which glucose is broken down to release energy in various organisms.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Three Pathways of Glucose Breakdown in Respiration:</strong></p>\n<p>The first step in all living organisms is <strong>Glycolysis</strong>, occurring in the <strong>Cytoplasm</strong>, where 1 molecule of 6-carbon Glucose is broken down into two 3-carbon molecules of <strong>Pyruvate</strong> with a net gain of 2 ATP.</p>\n\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Pathway 1: In the Absence of Oxygen (Anaerobic Respiration / Fermentation in Yeast):</strong><br>\n    $$\\text{Pyruvate} \\xrightarrow{\\text{In Yeast (Fermentation)}} 2\\text{ Ethanol} (\\text{C}_2\\text{H}_5\\text{OH}) + 2\\text{CO}_2 + \\text{Energy (2 ATP)}$$\n    Incomplete breakdown yielding ethanol and carbon dioxide.\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Pathway 2: In Lack of Oxygen (In Human Skeletal Muscle Cells during Vigorous Exercise):</strong><br>\n    $$\\text{Pyruvate} \\xrightarrow{\\text{Lack of O}_2 \\text{ in muscles}} 2\\text{ Lactic acid} (\\text{C}_3\\text{H}_6\\text{O}_3) + \\text{Energy (2 ATP)}$$\n    The accumulation of lactic acid crystals inside fatigued muscle fibers causes painful <strong>muscle cramps</strong>.\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Pathway 3: In the Presence of Oxygen (Aerobic Respiration in Mitochondria):</strong><br>\n    $$\\text{Pyruvate} + \\text{O}_2 \\xrightarrow{\\text{Mitochondria (Krebs cycle)}} 6\\text{CO}_2 + 6\\text{H}_2\\text{O} + \\text{Energy (36 to 38 ATP)}$$\n    Complete aerobic oxidation yielding carbon dioxide, water, and an enormous amount of metabolic energy.\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Glycolysis in cytoplasm: Glucose -> Pyruvate</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Pathway 1 (Yeast: ethanol + CO2) and Pathway 2 (Muscles: lactic acid)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Pathway 3 (Mitochondria aerobic: CO2 + H2O + 38 ATP)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch5-q4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch5-q4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Describe double circulation of blood in human beings. Why is it necessary? Differentiate between arteries and veins.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[5 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Double Circulation in the Human Body:</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>1. Definition:</strong></p>\n<p>In human beings and other birds and mammals, blood travels through the heart <strong>twice</strong> during one complete cardiac cycle. Hence, it is termed <strong>Double Circulation</strong>. It consists of two distinct circulatory pathways:</p>\n\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(a) Pulmonary Circulation:</strong><br>\n    Deoxygenated blood collected from body tissues enters the Right Atrium $\\to$ passes to Right Ventricle $\\to$ pumped via <strong>Pulmonary Artery</strong> to the <strong>Lungs</strong> for oxygenation. Oxygenated blood from the lungs returns via <strong>Pulmonary Veins</strong> into the Left Atrium.\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(b) Systemic Circulation:</strong><br>\n    Oxygen-rich blood passes from Left Atrium $\\to$ Left Ventricle $\\to$ pumped under high pressure through the <strong>Aorta</strong> to all systemic body organs and tissues. Deoxygenated blood loaded with metabolic $\\text{CO}_2$ is returned via Superior and Inferior <strong>Vena Cava</strong> into the Right Atrium.\n  </li>\n</ol>\n\n<p><strong>2. Why Double Circulation is Necessary in Humans:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Complete Separation of Oxygenated and Deoxygenated Blood:</strong> The 4-chambered heart (two atria, two ventricles) completely prevents the intermixing of oxygen-rich and oxygen-poor blood.</li>\n  <li><strong>High Oxygen Delivery Efficiency:</strong> Birds and mammals are <strong>warm-blooded (endothermic)</strong> animals. They require an enormous amount of cellular energy to constantly maintain their constant core body temperature ($37^\\circ\\text{C}$ in humans) regardless of external environmental temperature fluctuations. Double circulation ensures a rapid and highly pressurized supply of oxygen to all cells.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>3. Structural Differences: Arteries vs Veins:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin:10px 0; font-size:0.88rem;\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"background:#f1f5f9; text-align:left;\">\n      <th style=\"padding:6px 10px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Feature</th>\n      <th style=\"padding:6px 10px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Arteries</th>\n      <th style=\"padding:6px 10px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Veins</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:6px 10px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Direction of Flow</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:6px 10px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Carry blood <strong>away from heart</strong> to body organs.</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:6px 10px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Carry blood <strong>towards heart</strong> from body organs.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:6px 10px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Vessel Walls</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:6px 10px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Thick, highly elastic and muscular walls (withstand high pressure).</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:6px 10px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Thin, less muscular walls (blood under low pressure).</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:6px 10px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Internal Valves</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:6px 10px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Valves are absent (blood flows under high ventricular pressure).</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:6px 10px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Have <strong>semilunar valves</strong> to prevent backflow of blood.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Defining double circulation: Pulmonary circulation pathway</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Systemic circulation pathway from left ventricle via aorta</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Explaining physiological necessity: thermoregulation and high energy demand</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Structural differentiation between arteries and veins</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch5-q5\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch5-q5')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Describe the structure and functioning of a nephron. Explain the three steps involved in the formation of urine in human kidneys.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[5 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Structure and Functioning of the Nephron:</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>Nephron</strong> is the fundamental microscopic structural and functional filtration unit of the human kidney. Each human kidney contains approximately 1 to 1.2 million nephrons.</p>\n\n<p><strong>1. Structural Components of a Nephron:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Bowman's Capsule:</strong> A cup-shaped double-walled sac located in the renal cortex.</li>\n  <li><strong>Glomerulus:</strong> A dense tuft of microscopic blood capillaries enclosed within Bowman's capsule, supplied by an afferent renal arteriole and drained by an efferent arteriole. Bowman's capsule and Glomerulus together form the <strong>Malpighian corpuscle</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Renal Tubule:</strong> A long, highly coiled tubule consisting of:\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Proximal Convoluted Tubule (PCT)</em></li>\n      <li><em>Loop of Henle</em> (hairpin loop descending into renal medulla)</li>\n      <li><em>Distal Convoluted Tubule (DCT)</em></li>\n      <li><em>Collecting Duct</em>, which drains into the renal pelvis and ureter.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n\n<p><strong>2. Three Steps of Urine Formation:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Ultrafiltration (Glomerular Filtration):</strong><br>\n    The diameter of the afferent arteriole is wider than that of the efferent arteriole. This creates very high hydrostatic pressure inside the glomerulus. Blood is filtered across the capillary endothelial pores and podocyte filtration slits into Bowman's capsule space. The resulting <strong>initial filtrate</strong> (about 180 litres/day in a healthy adult) contains water, glucose, amino acids, mineral salts, urea, and uric acid, but is free of blood cells and plasma proteins.\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Selective Reabsorption:</strong><br>\n    As the filtrate flows down the PCT and Loop of Henle, essential nutrients are selectively reabsorbed back into the peritubular capillaries:\n    <ul>\n      <li>$100\\%$ of <strong>glucose</strong> and <strong>amino acids</strong> are actively reabsorbed.</li>\n      <li>Major fractions of $\\text{Na}^+, \\text{K}^+$, and mineral ions are reabsorbed.</li>\n      <li>Water is reabsorbed by osmosis under the control of <strong>Antidiuretic Hormone (ADH)</strong>. Out of 180 L of filtrate, approximately <strong>$1.5 - 2\\text{ litres}$</strong> is excreted daily as concentrated urine.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Tubular Secretion:</strong><br>\n    Additional metabolic wastes, extra $\\text{K}^+$ ions, $\\text{H}^+$ ions, and drug residues are actively secreted from peritubular capillaries into the tubular fluid in DCT, maintaining electrolyte balance and physiological blood pH ($\\approx 7.4$).\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Describing structure: Bowman's capsule, Glomerulus, PCT, Loop of Henle, DCT, Collecting duct</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Ultrafiltration: high glomerular pressure and composition of initial filtrate</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Selective reabsorption of glucose, amino acids, water and tubular secretion</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case Study Questions (HOTS) — Chapter 5</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #34d399;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Case Study Question • Renal Failure & Dialysis</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\">A patient suffering from long-term uncontrolled diabetes developed end-stage chronic kidney disease (CKD) with high blood urea levels. The nephrologist put the patient on hemodialysis. (i) What is an artificial kidney (hemodialyzer)? (ii) How does the dialysis fluid differ from blood plasma? (iii) What important step of natural urine formation is absent in hemodialysis?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(i) Artificial Kidney:</strong> A biomedical device used to filter nitrogenous metabolic waste products (urea, creatinine, uric acid) from the patient's blood when the biological kidneys fail.</li>\n  <li><strong>(ii) Dialysing Fluid Composition:</strong> The dialysing fluid has the same osmotic pressure and identical concentration of essential salts, glucose, and water as normal blood plasma, but it <strong>completely lacks nitrogenous wastes (urea)</strong>. Therefore, urea diffuses down the concentration gradient from blood through semi-permeable cellophane tubes into the dialysing fluid.</li>\n  <li><strong>(iii) Missing Step:</strong> <strong>Selective reabsorption</strong> is completely absent in an artificial kidney because the dialysing system operates by passive diffusion without physiological cellular transporters.</li>\n</ol></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(4)\">← Previous Chapter</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(6)\">Next Chapter →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "6": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch6\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">6</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">Science • Class 10 Board Exam (NCERT Rationalized)</div>\n      <h2>Chapter 6: Control and Coordination</h2>\n      <p>Nervous System, Neuron Structure, Reflex Arc, Human Brain & Endocrine Hormones — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text Questions (Page 105, 108, 114)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch6-q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch6-q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Draw the structure of a neuron and explain how nerve impulses are transmitted from one neuron to another across a synapse.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Structure and Nerve Impulse Transmission in a Neuron:</strong></p>\n<p>A <strong>Neuron</strong> is the specialized structural and functional conducting unit of the nervous system.</p>\n\n<p><strong>1. Structural Parts of a Neuron:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Dendrites:</strong> Fine, branching cytoplasmic projections that detect environmental stimuli and receive signals from sensory receptors or neighboring neurons.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cell Body (Cyton / Soma):</strong> Contains the nucleus, neuroplasm, and organelles; integrates incoming graded potentials.</li>\n  <li><strong>Axon:</strong> A long, cylindrical cytoplasmic fiber insulated by a fatty <strong>myelin sheath</strong> that rapidly conducts electrical impulses away from the cell body toward synaptic terminals.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<div class=\"sci-diagram-wrap\" style=\"margin: 18px auto; text-align: center; max-width: 480px; background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #d1fae5; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; box-shadow: 0 2px 8px rgba(5,150,105,0.06);\">\n  <svg viewBox=\"0 0 400 180\" width=\"100%\" height=\"160\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <!-- Dendrites -->\n  <g stroke=\"#059669\" stroke-width=\"2\">\n    <line x1=\"80\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"30\" y2=\"40\"/><line x1=\"80\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"25\" y2=\"80\"/><line x1=\"80\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"35\" y2=\"120\"/>\n    <line x1=\"30\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"15\" y2=\"30\"/><line x1=\"30\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"20\" y2=\"55\"/>\n    <line x1=\"35\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"20\" y2=\"135\"/>\n  </g>\n  <!-- Cell body Cyton -->\n  <polygon points=\"80,50 115,70 120,95 85,115 65,85\" fill=\"#ecfdf5\" stroke=\"#059669\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/>\n  <circle cx=\"92\" cy=\"83\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#047857\"/>\n  <text x=\"92\" y=\"87\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"9\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">N</text>\n  <!-- Axon -->\n  <line x1=\"120\" y1=\"85\" x2=\"330\" y2=\"85\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"4\"/>\n  <!-- Myelin Sheaths -->\n  <rect x=\"140\" y=\"76\" width=\"35\" height=\"18\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#bfdbfe\" stroke=\"#1d4ed8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <rect x=\"190\" y=\"76\" width=\"35\" height=\"18\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#bfdbfe\" stroke=\"#1d4ed8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <rect x=\"240\" y=\"76\" width=\"35\" height=\"18\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#bfdbfe\" stroke=\"#1d4ed8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <rect x=\"290\" y=\"76\" width=\"30\" height=\"18\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#bfdbfe\" stroke=\"#1d4ed8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <!-- Nerve endings -->\n  <g stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\">\n    <line x1=\"330\" y1=\"85\" x2=\"370\" y2=\"55\"/><line x1=\"330\" y1=\"85\" x2=\"375\" y2=\"85\"/><line x1=\"330\" y1=\"85\" x2=\"370\" y2=\"115\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"370\" cy=\"55\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/><circle cx=\"375\" cy=\"85\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/><circle cx=\"370\" cy=\"115\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  </g>\n  <!-- Labels -->\n  <text x=\"35\" y=\"22\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#059669\">Dendrites</text>\n  <text x=\"92\" y=\"135\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#059669\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Cell Body (Cyton)</text>\n  <text x=\"215\" y=\"65\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1d4ed8\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Axon (Myelin Sheath)</text>\n  <text x=\"360\" y=\"140\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2563eb\">Nerve Endings</text>\n</svg>\n  <div style=\"font-size: 0.8rem; font-weight: 600; color: #065f46; margin-top: 8px;\">Figure: Diagram of a Neuron showing Dendrites, Cell Body, Axon, and Nerve Endings</div>\n</div>\n<p><strong>2. How Impulse Travels & Synapse Transmission:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Generation:</em> Information received at the dendritic tip sets off a local chemical reaction that creates an <strong>electrical impulse (action potential)</strong>.</li>\n  <li><em>Conduction:</em> The electrical impulse travels rapidly through the cyton and along the length of the axon to its terminal end.</li>\n  <li><em>At the Synapse:</em> A <strong>synapse</strong> is a microscopic junction between the axonal terminal of one neuron and the dendrite of the next neuron. When the electrical impulse reaches the axonal tip, it triggers the release of specialized chemical messengers called <strong>neurotransmitters</strong> (e.g., Acetylcholine) into the synaptic cleft.</li>\n  <li>These chemical molecules diffuse across the synaptic gap and bind to specific receptors on the dendrite of the adjacent neuron, generating a fresh electrical impulse. This chemical step ensures <strong>strictly one-way (unidirectional) transmission</strong> of nerve impulses.</li>\n</ul>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Structure of neuron: dendrites, cell body, axon with myelin sheath</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Electrical impulse conduction along axon</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Chemical transmission across synapse via neurotransmitters ensuring unidirectional flow</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch6-q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch6-q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What is a reflex action? Trace the path of a reflex arc when you accidentally touch a hot object.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Reflex Action and Reflex Arc:</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>1. Definition:</strong></p>\n<p>A <strong>Reflex Action</strong> is a rapid, automatic, involuntary, and immediate motor response to an environmental stimulus that occurs without conscious thought or intervention of the cerebrum (e.g. instantly withdrawing hand on touching a boiling hot pan, knee-jerk reflex).</p>\n\n<p><strong>2. Sequence of Components in a Reflex Arc:</strong></p>\n<p>The neural pathway traversed by nerve impulses during a reflex action is called a <strong>Reflex Arc</strong>:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Receptor:</strong> Sensory organ detecting the stimulus (e.g. thermoreceptors in finger skin detecting heat).</li>\n  <li><strong>Sensory Neuron (Afferent neuron):</strong> Carries electrical nerve impulses from the receptor to the spinal cord.</li>\n  <li><strong>Relay Neuron (Interneuron):</strong> Located in the grey matter of the <strong>spinal cord</strong>; processes the sensory signal and immediately transfers the impulse to a motor neuron.</li>\n  <li><strong>Motor Neuron (Efferent neuron):</strong> Transmits the motor command impulse from the spinal cord to the target effector organ.</li>\n  <li><strong>Effector:</strong> Target muscle or gland (e.g. biceps muscle of arm) that contracts to pull the hand away instantly from danger.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"sci-diagram-wrap\" style=\"margin: 18px auto; text-align: center; max-width: 480px; background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #d1fae5; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; box-shadow: 0 2px 8px rgba(5,150,105,0.06);\">\n  <svg viewBox=\"0 0 380 200\" width=\"100%\" height=\"175\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <!-- Heat Source & Hand (Receptor) -->\n  <circle cx=\"50\" cy=\"100\" r=\"22\" fill=\"#fed7aa\" stroke=\"#ea580c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"50\" y=\"104\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#9a3412\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Receptor (Skin)</text>\n  <!-- Sensory Neuron Pathway (Red) -->\n  <path d=\"M 72 90 Q 160 40 260 80\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\"/>\n  <text x=\"160\" y=\"48\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#dc2626\">Sensory Neuron</text>\n  <!-- Spinal Cord (Relay) -->\n  <rect x=\"260\" y=\"60\" width=\"80\" height=\"80\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#e0e7ff\" stroke=\"#4f46e5\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"92\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#312e81\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Spinal Cord</text>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"112\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"9\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#4f46e5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">(Relay Neuron)</text>\n  <!-- Motor Neuron Pathway (Green) -->\n  <path d=\"M 260 120 Q 160 160 72 110\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#059669\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/>\n  <text x=\"160\" y=\"165\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#059669\">Motor Neuron</text>\n  <text x=\"50\" y=\"145\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Effector (Muscle)</text>\n</svg>\n  <div style=\"font-size: 0.8rem; font-weight: 600; color: #065f46; margin-top: 8px;\">Figure: Schematic Pathway of a Reflex Arc (Receptor → Sensory → Spinal Cord → Motor → Effector)</div>\n</div>\n<p><em>Evolutionary Advantage:</em> Because thinking by the conscious brain is relatively slow, reflex arcs evolved in the spinal cord to provide rapid emergency responses to safeguard the body from severe physical trauma.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Definition of reflex action as rapid, involuntary, unconscious response</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Five sequential components of reflex arc: Receptor -> Sensory -> Relay/Spinal cord -> Motor -> Effector</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch6-q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch6-q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What are the major parts of the human brain? Mention the primary functions of cerebrum, cerebellum, and medulla oblongata.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[5 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Structure and Functional Regions of the Human Brain:</strong></p>\n<p>The human brain is housed safely inside the bony cranium (skull) and surrounded by three protective cerebrospinal fluid-filled membranes called <strong>meninges</strong>. It is divided into three primary regions:</p>\n\n<p><strong>1. Forebrain (Prosencephalon):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>The <strong>Cerebrum</strong> is the largest and most complex part of the brain.</li>\n  <li><strong>Key Functions:</strong>\n    <br>(a) Main site of conscious thinking, reasoning, logic, memory, intelligence, and speech.\n    <br>(b) Sensory processing areas for receiving visual, auditory, olfactory (smell), gustatory (taste), and touch sensations.\n    <br>(c) Initiates voluntary muscular movements (walking, writing, speaking).\n    <br>(d) Contains specialized association centres and hunger/satiety centers.\n  </li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>2. Midbrain (Mesencephalon):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Connects forebrain to hindbrain.</li>\n  <li>Controls <strong>involuntary ocular reflexes</strong> (pupil dilation, eye movement) and auditory reflexes in response to sudden visual or sound changes.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>3. Hindbrain (Rhombencephalon):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>(a) Cerebellum:</strong> Responsible for <strong>precision of voluntary actions</strong> (threading a needle, walking in a straight line, riding a bicycle) and maintaining body <strong>posture, balance, and equilibrium</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>(b) Medulla Oblongata:</strong> Controls vital involuntary life autonomic reflexes including <strong>blood pressure, salivation, heartbeat, swallowing, coughing, and vomiting</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>(c) Pons:</strong> Participates in the regulation and rhythm of <strong>respiration</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Forebrain (Cerebrum): thinking, memory, intelligence, voluntary control, sensory areas</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Midbrain: visual and auditory reflex movements</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Hindbrain: Cerebellum (posture/balance), Medulla (involuntary BP/vomiting), Pons (breathing)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch6-q4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch6-q4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Name the plant hormones responsible for the following: (i) Growth of stem, (ii) Promotion of cell division, (iii) Bending of shoot towards light, (iv) Wilting of leaves.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Major Phytohormones (Plant Hormones) and their Biological Functions:</strong></p>\n\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>1. Auxins:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Synthesized at growing shoot and root apices.</li>\n      <li>Promote <strong>cell elongation</strong> and stem growth.</li>\n      <li>Mediate <strong>Phototropism</strong>: When unidirectional light shines on a shoot, auxins diffuse to the shaded side, stimulating cells on the darker side to elongate faster, causing the shoot to bend towards light.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>2. Gibberellins:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Promote <strong>stem elongation</strong> and breaking seed/bud dormancy.</li>\n      <li>Promote fruit growth.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>3. Cytokinins:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Promote rapid <strong>cell division (cytokinesis)</strong>.</li>\n      <li>Present in high concentrations in regions of rapid cell division (fruits and seeds); delay leaf senescence.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>4. Abscisic Acid (ABA):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Acts as a <strong>growth inhibitor</strong> (stress hormone).</li>\n      <li>Causes <strong>wilting and falling of leaves</strong> (abscission) and induces stomatal closure during water drought.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Auxins: synthesis at shoot tips, cell elongation, and phototropism</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Gibberellins: stem growth and Cytokinins: cell division in fruits/seeds</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Abscisic acid: growth inhibition, leaf wilting, and stomatal closure</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch6-q5\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch6-q5')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why is the use of iodised salt advisable? Name the disease caused by deficiency of iodine in our diet.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Physiological Importance of Iodised Salt:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Hormone Synthesis:</strong> The <strong>Thyroid gland</strong> (located in the neck) requires <strong>Iodine</strong> as an indispensable trace element to synthesize the hormone <strong>Thyroxine ($\\text{T}_4$)</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Function of Thyroxine:</strong> Thyroxine regulates the body's basal carbohydrate, protein, and fat <strong>metabolism</strong>, providing optimal energy balance and ensuring balanced physical and mental growth in human beings.</li>\n  <li><strong>Consequence of Iodine Deficiency:</strong> If our diet is deficient in iodine, the thyroid gland cannot synthesize sufficient thyroxine. In an attempt to compensate and absorb more iodine from the blood, the thyroid gland hypertrophies and enlarges abnormally, causing a swollen neck condition known as <strong>Simple Goitre</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Using iodised table salt (salt fortified with potassium iodate $\\text{KIO}_3$) ensures an adequate daily supply of iodine to prevent goitre and support healthy brain development.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Iodine needed by thyroid gland to synthesize thyroxine</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Function of thyroxine in regulating carbohydrate, protein, fat metabolism</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Explaining goitre (swollen neck) resulting from deficiency and prevention by iodised salt</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case Study Questions (HOTS) — Chapter 6</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #34d399;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Case Study Question • Diabetes & Hormone Regulation</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\">A 50-year-old person experiences frequent urination, excessive thirst, and unexplained weight loss. Blood tests reveal an elevated fasting blood glucose level of 240 mg/dL. The doctor diagnoses Diabetes Mellitus. (i) Name the hormone and the endocrine gland responsible for regulating blood glucose levels. (ii) Why are some diabetic patients treated with daily insulin injections? (iii) What feedback mechanism controls insulin secretion?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(i) Hormone and Gland:</strong> Hormone is <strong>Insulin</strong>, secreted by the beta cells of the Islets of Langerhans in the <strong>Pancreas</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>(ii) Insulin Injections:</strong> In diabetic patients, the pancreas produces insufficient insulin or cells become resistant to it. Glucose cannot be absorbed into body cells or converted into glycogen in the liver, remaining elevated in the blood. Daily insulin injections supply the missing hormone, promoting cellular glucose uptake and normalizing blood sugar.</li>\n  <li><strong>(iii) Feedback Mechanism:</strong> When blood glucose levels rise after a meal, the pancreatic beta cells detect the surge and secrete more insulin. As blood glucose falls back to normal range, insulin secretion is automatically downregulated. This self-regulating system is called a <strong>negative feedback mechanism</strong>.</li>\n</ol></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(5)\">← Previous Chapter</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(7)\">Next Chapter →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "7": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch7\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">7</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">Science • Class 10 Board Exam (NCERT Rationalized)</div>\n      <h2>Chapter 7: How do Organisms Reproduce?</h2>\n      <p>Asexual Modes, Sexual Reproduction in Plants, Human Reproductive Anatomy, Menstruation & Contraception — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text Questions (Page 128, 133, 140)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch7-q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch7-q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What is the importance of DNA copying in reproduction? Why is variation beneficial to the species but not necessarily for the individual?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Biological Significance of DNA Copying in Reproduction:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>1. Transmission of Genetic Information:</strong><br>\n    The chromosomes in the nucleus of a cell contain genetic information for the inheritance of features from parents to next generation in the form of <strong>DNA (Deoxyribonucleic Acid)</strong>. DNA copying ensures that both daughter cells receive an exact set of genetic blueprints necessary for structural body design.\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>2. Generation of Organic Cellular Apparatus:</strong><br>\n    DNA copying is accompanied by the creation of an additional cellular apparatus (cytoplasm, organelles) so that when the cell divides, each daughter cell has functional machinery to survive independently.\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>3. Subtle Inbuilt Variations for Species Survival:</strong><br>\n    No biochemical copying mechanism is $100\\%$ completely error-free. DNA replication introduces subtle nucleotide variations (mutations). While extreme variations can be lethal, subtle variations provide the raw material for <strong>evolution and adaptation</strong>, enabling populations of a species to withstand sudden environmental catastrophes (such as global warming, drought, or epidemics).\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Transmission of genetic information from parent to offspring</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Creation of functional cellular apparatus alongside DNA duplication</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Role of subtle variations in natural selection, adaptation, and species survival</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch7-q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch7-q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How does binary fission differ from multiple fission? Give one example of each.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Comparison: Binary Fission vs Multiple Fission</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin:12px 0; font-size:0.88rem;\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"background:#f1f5f9; text-align:left;\">\n      <th style=\"padding:8px 12px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Feature</th>\n      <th style=\"padding:8px 12px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Binary Fission</th>\n      <th style=\"padding:8px 12px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Multiple Fission</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px 12px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Number of Daughter Cells</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px 12px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">The parent unicellular organism divides into <strong>two identical daughter cells</strong>.</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px 12px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">The parent organism divides simultaneously into <strong>many daughter cells</strong>.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px 12px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Environmental Condition</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px 12px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Occurs during <strong>favourable conditions</strong> of food, moisture, and temperature.</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px 12px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Commonly occurs during <strong>unfavourable conditions</strong> (protected by a thick cyst wall).</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px 12px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Examples</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px 12px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><em>Amoeba proteus</em>, <em>Paramecium</em>, <em>Leishmania</em> (has whip-like flagellum).</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px 12px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><em>Plasmodium</em> (the malarial parasite), encysted <em>Amoeba</em>.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Contrasting number of daughter cells (2 vs many)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Environmental conditions (favourable vs unfavourable with cyst)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Correct examples (Amoeba/Leishmania vs Plasmodium)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch7-q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch7-q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Explain the process of sexual reproduction in flowering plants from pollination to seed formation. What are post-fertilisation changes?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[5 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Sexual Reproduction and Seed Formation in Angiosperms (Flowering Plants):</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>1. Reproductive Parts of a Flower:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Stamen (Male reproductive organ):</strong> Consists of <strong>anther</strong> (produces yellow haploid pollen grains containing male gametes) and filament.</li>\n  <li><strong>Carpel / Pistil (Female reproductive organ):</strong> Consists of sticky <strong>stigma</strong> (receives pollen), slender <strong>style</strong>, and swollen basal <strong>ovary</strong> (contains ovules, each housing an egg cell).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>2. Pollination:</strong></p>\n<p>The transfer of pollen grains from anther to stigma is called <strong>pollination</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Self-pollination:</em> Transfer occurs within the same flower or between flowers of the same plant.</li>\n  <li><em>Cross-pollination:</em> Transfer occurs between flowers of different plants of the same species, facilitated by abiotic agents (wind, water) or biotic vectors (insects, birds, bats).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>3. Fertilisation and Pollen Tube Growth:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Upon landing on a compatible receptive stigma, the pollen grain germinates and develops a long <strong>pollen tube</strong>.</li>\n  <li>The pollen tube grows through the tissues of the style and reaches the ovule in the ovary via the micropyle.</li>\n  <li>Two male gametes are discharged into the embryo sac:\n    <ul>\n      <li>One male gamete fuses with the female egg cell to form a diploid <strong>Zygote</strong> (Syngamy).</li>\n      <li>The second male gamete fuses with the two polar nuclei to form the triploid <strong>Endosperm</strong> (Triple fusion). This unique event is known as <strong>Double Fertilisation</strong>.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n\n<p><strong>4. Post-Fertilisation Changes:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>The zygote divides repeatedly by mitosis to develop into an <strong>Embryo</strong>.</li>\n  <li>The ovule develops a tough protective integument coat and converts into a <strong>Seed</strong>.</li>\n  <li>The ovary grows rapidly, ripens, and transforms into the <strong>Fruit</strong>. Petals, sepals, and stamens shrivel and fall off.</li>\n</ul>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Description of stamen and carpel parts</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Pollination (self vs cross) and pollen tube germination down the style</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Double fertilisation mechanism (syngamy and triple fusion)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Post-fertilisation changes: ovule -> seed and ovary -> fruit</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch7-q4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch7-q4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What happens when the egg is not fertilised in a human female? Explain the phenomenon of menstruation.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Mechanism of Menstruation in Human Females:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>In human females, one of the two ovaries releases one mature ovum (egg cell) every month (ovulation), roughly midway through a 28-day menstrual cycle.</li>\n  <li>In anticipation of receiving a fertilised egg (zygote), the <strong>uterine wall (endometrium)</strong> thickens, becomes richly vascularized, and spongy with blood vessels to nourish the prospective embryo.</li>\n  <li><strong>If Fertilisation does NOT occur:</strong><br>\n    The unfertilised egg survives for about 24 to 48 hours and then degenerates. As progesterone hormone levels plunge, the thickened, spongy endometrium is no longer required.\n  </li>\n  <li>The lining of the uterus slowly breaks down and sheds. Its shredded tissues along with blood and mucus are discharged through the vagina as <strong>menstrual flow (Menstruation)</strong>, which typically lasts for about <strong>3 to 5 days</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Monthly release of ovum and thickening of vascular uterine wall</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Fate of unfertilised egg and fall in hormone levels</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Breakdown and discharge of uterine endometrium, blood, and mucus (menstruation)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch7-q5\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch7-q5')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What are the different methods of contraception? State one advantage of barrier methods over other methods.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Categories of Contraceptive Methods:</strong></p>\n<p>Contraception refers to the deliberate prevention of unwanted pregnancy. Major methods include:</p>\n\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>1. Mechanical Barrier Methods:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Examples: <strong>Condoms</strong> (for males) and diaphragms/cervical caps (for females).</li>\n      <li><em>Mechanism:</em> Physically prevent sperm from reaching the female genital tract.</li>\n      <li><em>Dual Advantage:</em> Condoms provide crucial protection against <strong>Sexually Transmitted Diseases (STDs)</strong> such as HIV-AIDS, Syphilis, and Gonorrhoea.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>2. Chemical Methods (Oral Hormonal Pills):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Examples: Oral contraceptive pills (contain synthetic estrogen/progesterone).</li>\n      <li><em>Mechanism:</em> Alter the female hormonal balance to inhibit ovulation so that no egg is released.</li>\n      <li><em>Side effects:</em> May cause hormonal imbalance, nausea, or mood fluctuations.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>3. Intrauterine Contraceptive Devices (IUCDs):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Example: <strong>Copper-T</strong> placed inside the uterus by a trained medical professional.</li>\n      <li><em>Mechanism:</em> Releases copper ions that suppress sperm motility and fertilising capacity, preventing implantation.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>4. Surgical Methods (Permanent Sterilisation):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Vasectomy (in males):</strong> A small portion of the vas deferens is cut and tied to prevent sperm delivery.</li>\n      <li><strong>Tubectomy (in females):</strong> A small portion of the fallopian tubes is cut and ligated to block egg passage.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Barrier methods (condoms) and protection against STDs</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Chemical methods (hormonal oral pills inhibiting ovulation)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">IUCDs (Copper-T) and Surgical sterilisation (Vasectomy & Tubectomy)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case Study Questions (HOTS) — Chapter 7</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #34d399;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Case Study Question • Male Reproductive System</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\">The testes in human males are located outside the abdominal cavity within a pouch of skin called the scrotum. Furthermore, secretions of the prostate gland and seminal vesicles merge with sperm. (i) Why are testes located outside the abdominal cavity in the scrotum? (ii) What is the dual function of the male urethra? (iii) What is the role of seminal vesicle and prostate gland secretions?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(i) Role of Scrotum:</strong> <strong>Spermatogenesis</strong> (production of viable sperm) requires a temperature that is <strong>$2^\\circ\\text{C} - 2.5^\\circ\\text{C}$ lower</strong> than the internal human body core temperature ($37^\\circ\\text{C}$). The scrotum hangs outside the warm abdomen to maintain this cooler temperature.</li>\n  <li><strong>(ii) Dual Function of Urethra:</strong> In human males, the urethra serves as a <strong>common passage</strong> for both the discharge of urine from the urinary bladder and the ejaculation of semen containing sperm during reproduction.</li>\n  <li><strong>(iii) Role of Accessory Gland Secretions:</strong> The seminal vesicles and prostate gland add their alkaline secretions to sperm:\n    <ul>\n      <li>Provide a fluid medium for easy sperm motility and transport.</li>\n      <li>Provide nourishment (fructose, enzymes).</li>\n      <li>Neutralize the acidic environment of the urethra and vagina.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(6)\">← Previous Chapter</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(8)\">Next Chapter →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "8": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch8\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">8</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">Science • Class 10 Board Exam (NCERT Rationalized)</div>\n      <h2>Chapter 8: Heredity</h2>\n      <p>Inherited vs Acquired Traits, Mendel's Monohybrid & Dihybrid Crosses & Mechanism of Sex Determination — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text Questions (Page 143, 147)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch8-q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch8-q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How do inherited traits differ from acquired traits? Give two examples of each. Why are traits acquired during the lifetime of an individual not inherited?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Comparison: Inherited Traits vs Acquired Traits</strong></p>\n\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Inherited Traits:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>These are traits determined by specific <strong>genes located on DNA</strong> inside reproductive germ cells (gametes).</li>\n      <li>They are transmitted from parents to their progeny from generation to generation.</li>\n      <li><em>Examples:</em> Eye colour, skin pigmentation, natural hair texture, blood group, attached or free earlobes.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Acquired Traits:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>These are phenotypic characteristics developed by an individual during its lifetime in response to environmental conditions, learning, injury, or physical training.</li>\n      <li>They involve changes only in <strong>non-reproductive somatic cells</strong>, without altering the DNA nucleotide sequence of germ cells.</li>\n      <li>Therefore, acquired traits <strong>cannot be inherited</strong> by offspring.</li>\n      <li><em>Examples:</em> Muscular physique from bodybuilding, loss of a limb or tail in an accident, knowledge of swimming or playing a musical instrument. (e.g., Even if tails of mice are surgically removed for 20 successive generations, tailless mice are never born because the surgery does not alter germ cell DNA).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Definition of inherited traits with DNA alteration in germ cells and examples</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Definition of acquired traits with somatic limitation and explanation of why they cannot be passed on</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch8-q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch8-q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How did Mendel explain that traits may be dominant or recessive? Explain with the help of a monohybrid cross, showing F1 and F2 generations with phenotypic and genotypic ratios.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Mendel's Monohybrid Cross (Inheritance of One Pair of Contrasting Characters):</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>1. Cross Setup:</strong></p>\n<p>Mendel crossed pure-breeding homozygous <strong>Tall pea plants ($TT$)</strong> with pure-breeding homozygous <strong>Dwarf pea plants ($tt$)</strong>.</p>\n\n<p><strong>2. First Filial Generation ($F_1$):</strong></p>\n$$\\text{Parents: } TT \\times tt \\longrightarrow \\text{Gametes: } T \\text{ and } t$$\n$$F_1 \\text{ Progeny: } Tt \\quad (100\\% \\text{ Phenotypically Tall})$$\n<ul>\n  <li>Even though the plants inherited both factors ($T$ and $t$), only the Tall trait was expressed. Therefore, the trait for Tallness ($T$) is <strong>Dominant</strong>, whereas the dwarf trait ($t$) is <strong>Recessive</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>3. Second Filial Generation ($F_2$ by Self-pollination of $F_1$):</strong></p>\n$$\\text{Selfing } F_1: Tt \\times Tt$$\n<table style=\"width:240px; border-collapse:collapse; margin:10px 0; text-align:center; font-size:0.88rem;\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"background:#f1f5f9;\">\n      <th style=\"padding:6px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Gametes</th>\n      <th style=\"padding:6px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">$T$</th>\n      <th style=\"padding:6px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">$t$</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:6px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1; background:#f8fafc;\"><strong>$T$</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:6px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">$TT$ (Tall)</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:6px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">$Tt$ (Tall)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:6px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1; background:#f8fafc;\"><strong>$t$</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:6px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">$Tt$ (Tall)</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:6px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">$tt$ (Dwarf)</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<p><strong>4. $F_2$ Ratios:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Phenotypic Ratio:</strong> $\\mathbf{3 \\text{ Tall} : 1 \\text{ Dwarf}}$ ($75\\%$ Tall, $25\\%$ Dwarf).</li>\n  <li><strong>Genotypic Ratio:</strong> $\\mathbf{1\\, TT : 2\\, Tt : 1\\, tt}$ ($1 : 2 : 1$).</li>\n  <li><em>Conclusion:</em> Both dominant and recessive traits are inherited independently, but recessive traits are expressed only in the homozygous condition ($tt$).</li>\n</ul>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Parental cross TT x tt and F1 generation (all Tall Tt)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Punnett square for F2 generation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Stating phenotypic ratio 3:1 and genotypic ratio 1:2:1 with conclusions</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch8-q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch8-q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How did Mendel's experiments show that traits are inherited independently? Describe the dihybrid cross between round yellow and wrinkled green pea plants with the 9:3:3:1 ratio.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[5 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Mendel's Dihybrid Cross & Law of Independent Assortment:</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>1. Cross Setup:</strong></p>\n<p>Mendel crossed a pea plant having <strong>Round Yellow seeds ($RRYY$)</strong> with a plant having <strong>Wrinkled Green seeds ($rryy$)</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>$R$ = Round (dominant), $r$ = Wrinkled (recessive)</li>\n  <li>$Y$ = Yellow (dominant), $y$ = Green (recessive)</li>\n  <li>Gametes: $RY$ and $ry \\implies F_1$ Generation: $\\mathbf{RrYy}$ ($100\\%$ Round Yellow seeds).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>2. $F_2$ Generation (Selfing $RrYy \\times RrYy$):</strong></p>\n<p>Each $F_1$ plant produces 4 types of gametes in equal proportions: $RY, Ry, rY, ry$.</p>\n<p>A $4 \\times 4$ Punnett square yields 16 combinations:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Round Yellow ($R\\_Y\\_$):</strong> 9 plants</li>\n  <li><strong>Round Green ($R\\_yy$):</strong> 3 plants (New combination)</li>\n  <li><strong>Wrinkled Yellow ($rrY\\_$):</strong> 3 plants (New combination)</li>\n  <li><strong>Wrinkled Green ($rryy$):</strong> 1 plant</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p><strong>3. Classical $F_2$ Dihybrid Phenotypic Ratio:</strong></p>\n$$\\mathbf{9 : 3 : 3 : 1}$$\n\n<p><strong>4. Law of Independent Assortment:</strong></p>\n<p>The appearance of new recombinant phenotypes (Round Green and Wrinkled Yellow) proves that when two pairs of contrasting traits are combined in a hybrid, the segregation and assortment of one pair of characters is completely <strong>independent</strong> of the other pair of characters during gamete formation.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Parental setup RRYY x rryy and F1 hybrid RrYy</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Detailing 4 gamete types RY, Ry, rY, ry and 16 combinations</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Stating phenotypic ratio 9:3:3:1 with the 4 classes</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Statement and deduction of the Law of Independent Assortment</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch8-q4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch8-q4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">How is the sex of the child determined in human beings? Explain with a genetic cross diagram why women should not be blamed for giving birth to girl children.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Genetic Mechanism of Sex Determination in Human Beings:</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>1. Chromosome Complement:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Human somatic cells contain <strong>23 pairs (46 chromosomes)</strong>:\n    <ul>\n      <li>22 pairs are identical in both sexes and are called <strong>Autosomes</strong>.</li>\n      <li>1 pair (the 23rd pair) determines the sex and is called <strong>Sex Chromosomes (Allosomes)</strong>.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li>Human females possess a perfectly matched pair of sex chromosomes: $\\mathbf{XX}$.</li>\n  <li>Human males possess an unmatched pair consisting of one normal-sized $X$ and a shorter $Y$ chromosome: $\\mathbf{XY}$.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>2. Gamete Production & Cross:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>All female gametes (ova / eggs) carry only an $\\mathbf{X}$ chromosome ($22 + X$). Females are <em>homogametic</em>.</li>\n  <li>Male gametes (sperms) are of two types ($50\\%$ carry an $\\mathbf{X}$ chromosome, $50\\%$ carry a $\\mathbf{Y}$ chromosome). Males are <em>heterogametic</em>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Fertilisation Possibilities:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>If an ovum ($X$) is fertilised by an $X$-bearing sperm: Zygote is $\\mathbf{XX} \\longrightarrow$ <strong>Female child (Girl)</strong>.</li>\n      <li>If an ovum ($X$) is fertilised by a $Y$-bearing sperm: Zygote is $\\mathbf{XY} \\longrightarrow$ <strong>Male child (Boy)</strong>.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n\n<p><strong>3. Scientific Conclusion:</strong></p>\n<p>The probability of having a male or a female baby is strictly $50\\% : 50\\%$ ($1 : 1$). Because all eggs carry only an $X$ chromosome, it is the <strong>father's sperm ($X$ or $Y$)</strong> that determines the biological sex of the child. Holding the mother responsible for the birth of a girl child is completely scientifically baseless and unjust.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Chromosomal complement: 22 pairs autosomes, female XX, male XY</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Cross showing ovum (X) fertilised by X-sperm (XX girl) and Y-sperm (XY boy)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Statistical 50% probability and conclusion that father's sperm determines child's sex</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case Study Questions (HOTS) — Chapter 8</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #34d399;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Case Study Question • Blood Group Inheritance</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\">A man with blood group A marries a woman with blood group O and their daughter has blood group O. (i) Is this information enough to tell you which of the traits — blood group A or O — is dominant? (ii) What are the possible genotypes of the father and the daughter? (iii) If they have a second child, what is the probability that the child has blood group A?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(i) Sufficiency of Information:</strong> No, this single observation alone is not strictly sufficient to determine dominance without standard genetic theory. However, knowing that Blood group O is a recessive trait ($i$), the daughter can only have blood group O if she is homozygous recessive ($ii$), receiving one $i$ allele from the mother (who is $ii$) and one $i$ allele from the father. This implies that Blood group A is <strong>dominant ($I^A$)</strong> and Blood group O is <strong>recessive ($i$)</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>(ii) Genotypes:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Daughter (Blood group O): $\\mathbf{ii}$</li>\n      <li>Father (Blood group A): Heterozygous $\\mathbf{I^A i}$</li>\n      <li>Mother (Blood group O): $\\mathbf{ii}$</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(iii) Probability of Second Child having Blood Group A:</strong><br>\n    Cross: $I^A i \\times ii \\longrightarrow 50\\% \\, I^A i \\text{ (Blood group A)}$ and $50\\% \\, ii \\text{ (Blood group O)}$.<br>\n    The probability is <strong>$50\\%$ (or $1/2$)</strong>.\n  </li>\n</ol></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(7)\">← Previous Chapter</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(9)\">Next Chapter →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "9": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch9\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">9</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">Science • Class 10 Board Exam (NCERT Rationalized)</div>\n      <h2>Chapter 9: Light – Reflection and Refraction</h2>\n      <p>Spherical Mirrors, Mirror Formula, Refractive Index, Snell's Law, Lenses & Optical Power — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text Questions (Page 150, 155, 161, 166)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch9-q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch9-q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why do we prefer a convex mirror as a rear-view mirror in vehicles?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Optical Reasons for Using Convex Mirrors as Rear-View Mirrors in Vehicles:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Always Produces an Erect Image:</strong> A convex mirror always forms an <strong>erect (upright) and virtual image</strong> of objects placed anywhere in front of it, allowing the driver to see vehicles right-side up.</li>\n  <li><strong>Diminished Image Size:</strong> The image produced is always diminished (smaller in size), which allows distant and approaching vehicles to be seen clearly within the mirror's frame.</li>\n  <li><strong>Much Wider Field of View:</strong> Because convex mirrors are <strong>curved outwards</strong>, they have a vastly wider field of view compared to a plane mirror or concave mirror. This enables the driver to monitor a significantly larger area of traffic behind the vehicle, dramatically enhancing road safety during reversing and lane changes.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Always forms erect and virtual image</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Curved outwards giving a significantly wider field of view to the driver</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch9-q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch9-q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">An object, 4.0 cm in size, is placed at 25.0 cm in front of a concave mirror of focal length 15.0 cm. At what distance from the mirror should a screen be placed in order to obtain a sharp image? Find the nature and the size of the image.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Step-by-step Numerical Solution:</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>Step 1: Given Parameters (New Cartesian Sign Convention)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Object height: $h = +4.0\\text{ cm}$</li>\n  <li>Object distance: $u = -25.0\\text{ cm}$ (in front of mirror, negative)</li>\n  <li>Focal length of concave mirror: $f = -15.0\\text{ cm}$ (concave mirror focal point is in front, negative)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>Step 2: Calculate Image Distance ($v$) using Mirror Formula</strong></p>\n$$\\frac{1}{f} = \\frac{1}{v} + \\frac{1}{u} \\implies \\frac{1}{v} = \\frac{1}{f} - \\frac{1}{u}$$\n$$\\frac{1}{v} = \\frac{1}{-15} - \\frac{1}{-25} = -\\frac{1}{15} + \\frac{1}{25}$$\n<p>LCM of 15 and 25 is 75:</p>\n$$\\frac{1}{v} = \\frac{-5 + 3}{75} = -\\frac{2}{75}$$\n$$v = -\\frac{75}{2} = -37.5\\text{ cm}$$\n<p>The screen should be placed at a distance of <strong>$37.5\\text{ cm}$ in front of the mirror</strong>.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Step 3: Calculate Image Size ($h'$) and Nature using Magnification Formula</strong></p>\n$$m = -\\frac{v}{u} = \\frac{h'}{h}$$\n$$h' = -\\frac{v}{u} \\times h = -\\left(\\frac{-37.5}{-25}\\right) \\times 4.0 = -(1.5) \\times 4.0 = -6.0\\text{ cm}$$\n\n<div class=\"sci-diagram-wrap\" style=\"margin: 18px auto; text-align: center; max-width: 480px; background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #d1fae5; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; box-shadow: 0 2px 8px rgba(5,150,105,0.06);\">\n  <svg viewBox=\"0 0 380 200\" width=\"100%\" height=\"175\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <!-- Principal axis -->\n  <line x1=\"20\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"360\" y2=\"100\" stroke=\"#64748b\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <!-- Concave mirror arc -->\n  <path d=\"M 330 20 Q 310 100 330 180\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#0f172a\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n  <text x=\"340\" y=\"105\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#0f172a\">P</text>\n  <!-- Points F and C -->\n  <circle cx=\"240\" cy=\"100\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#dc2626\"/><text x=\"240\" y=\"118\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#dc2626\" text-anchor=\"middle\">F</text>\n  <circle cx=\"150\" cy=\"100\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/><text x=\"150\" y=\"118\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2563eb\" text-anchor=\"middle\">C</text>\n  <!-- Object between C and F -->\n  <line x1=\"195\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"195\" y2=\"55\" stroke=\"#059669\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n  <polygon points=\"195,50 191,58 199,58\" fill=\"#059669\"/>\n  <text x=\"195\" y=\"42\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#059669\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Object AB</text>\n  <!-- Parallel Ray to Mirror then through F -->\n  <line x1=\"195\" y1=\"55\" x2=\"315\" y2=\"55\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n  <line x1=\"315\" y1=\"55\" x2=\"80\" y2=\"175\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n  <!-- Ray through F reflected parallel -->\n  <line x1=\"195\" y1=\"55\" x2=\"240\" y2=\"100\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n  <line x1=\"240\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"315\" y2=\"145\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n  <line x1=\"315\" y1=\"145\" x2=\"80\" y2=\"145\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/>\n  <!-- Inverted Real Magnified Image beyond C -->\n  <line x1=\"100\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#b91c1c\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n  <polygon points=\"100,165 96,157 104,157\" fill=\"#b91c1c\"/>\n  <text x=\"100\" y=\"180\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#b91c1c\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Image A'B' (Real, Inverted)</text>\n</svg>\n  <div style=\"font-size: 0.8rem; font-weight: 600; color: #065f46; margin-top: 8px;\">Figure: Ray Diagram of Concave Mirror with Object between C and F Forming Image Beyond C</div>\n</div>\n<p><strong>Characteristics of the Image:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Position:</strong> At $37.5\\text{ cm}$ in front of the mirror (on the same side as the object).</li>\n  <li><strong>Nature:</strong> <strong>Real and Inverted</strong> (negative sign of $v$ and $h'$).</li>\n  <li><strong>Size:</strong> <strong>Enlarged / Magnified</strong> (height $= 6.0\\text{ cm}$, $|m| = 1.5 > 1$).</li>\n</ul>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Listing given values with correct Cartesian sign conventions: u = -25 cm, f = -15 cm</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Applying mirror formula to correctly find image distance v = -37.5 cm</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Calculating magnification and image height h' = -6.0 cm; stating real, inverted, enlarged nature</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch9-q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch9-q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">State the laws of refraction of light. Light enters from air to glass having refractive index 1.50. What is the speed of light in the glass? (Speed of light in vacuum is $3 \\times 10^8\\text{ m/s}$).</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Laws of Refraction of Light:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>First Law:</strong> The incident ray, the refracted ray and the normal to the interface of two transparent media at the point of incidence, all lie in the same plane.</li>\n  <li><strong>Second Law (Snell's Law of Refraction):</strong><br>\n    The ratio of the sine of the angle of incidence to the sine of the angle of refraction is a constant for the light of a given colour and for the given pair of media:\n    $$\\frac{\\sin i}{\\sin r} = \\text{constant} = n_{21} = \\frac{n_2}{n_1}$$\n  </li>\n</ol>\n\n<p><strong>Numerical Calculation of Speed of Light in Glass:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Absolute refractive index of glass: $n_g = 1.50$</li>\n  <li>Speed of light in vacuum: $c = 3 \\times 10^8\\text{ m/s}$</li>\n</ul>\n$$n_g = \\frac{c}{v_g} \\implies v_g = \\frac{c}{n_g}$$\n$$v_g = \\frac{3 \\times 10^8\\text{ m/s}}{1.50} = 2.0 \\times 10^8\\text{ m/s}$$\n<p><strong>Final Answer:</strong> The speed of light in glass is <strong>$2 \\times 10^8\\text{ m/s}$</strong>.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Statement of the two laws of refraction including Snell's Law formula</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Formula n = c/v and computation of speed of light in glass = 2 * 10^8 m/s</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch9-q4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch9-q4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">A concave lens has focal length of 15 cm. At what distance should the object from the lens be placed so that it forms an image at 10 cm from the lens? Also, find the magnification produced by the lens and define power of a lens.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Step-by-step Numerical Solution:</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>Step 1: Given Parameters</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Focal length of concave lens: $f = -15\\text{ cm}$ (concave lens has virtual focus, negative)</li>\n  <li>Image distance: $v = -10\\text{ cm}$ (concave lens always forms a virtual image on the same side as the object, negative)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>Step 2: Calculate Object Distance ($u$) using Lens Formula</strong></p>\n$$\\frac{1}{f} = \\frac{1}{v} - \\frac{1}{u} \\implies \\frac{1}{u} = \\frac{1}{v} - \\frac{1}{f}$$\n$$\\frac{1}{u} = \\frac{1}{-10} - \\frac{1}{-15} = -\\frac{1}{10} + \\frac{1}{15}$$\n<p>LCM of 10 and 15 is 30:</p>\n$$\\frac{1}{u} = \\frac{-3 + 2}{30} = -\\frac{1}{30} \\implies u = -30\\text{ cm}$$\n<p>The object should be placed at a distance of <strong>$30\\text{ cm}$ in front of the lens</strong>.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Step 3: Calculate Magnification ($m$)</strong></p>\n$$m = \\frac{v}{u} = \\frac{-10\\text{ cm}}{-30\\text{ cm}} = +\\frac{1}{3} \\approx +0.33$$\n<p>The positive sign indicates an <strong>erect and virtual</strong> image, diminished to one-third of the object's height.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Step 4: Power of a Lens Definition & Formula:</strong></p>\n<p>The power of a lens ($P$) is defined as the reciprocal of its focal length expressed in metres. It measures the degree of convergence or divergence of light rays:</p>\n$$P = \\frac{1}{f\\text{ (in metres)}}$$\n<p>The SI unit of power of a lens is <strong>Dioptre (D)</strong>. $1\\text{ D} = 1\\text{ m}^{-1}$.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Writing lens formula with proper sign convention f = -15 cm, v = -10 cm</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Solving for object distance u = -30 cm and magnification m = +1/3</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Definition of power of lens P = 1/f(m) with SI unit Dioptre (D)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch9-q5\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch9-q5')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Explain the New Cartesian Sign Convention for reflection and refraction. Compare mirror formula with lens formula, and find the net power and focal length of a combination of lenses of powers +3.5 D and -1.5 D placed in contact.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[5 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Comparison of Optical Instruments: Spherical Mirrors vs Spherical Lenses</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>1. Cartesian Sign Conventions:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Object is always placed to the left of the optical centre/pole: $u$ is always <strong>negative</strong>.</li>\n  <li>All distances measured in the direction of incident light are positive; opposite to incident light are negative.</li>\n  <li>Distances measured upward perpendicular to the principal axis are positive; downward are negative.</li>\n  <li><strong>Focal Lengths:</strong>\n    <br>• Concave mirror & Concave lens: $f$ is <strong>negative ($-$)</strong>.\n    <br>• Convex mirror & Convex lens: $f$ is <strong>positive ($+$)</strong>.\n  </li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>2. Formula Comparison:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin:12px 0; font-size:0.88rem;\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"background:#f1f5f9; text-align:left;\">\n      <th style=\"padding:8px 12px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Parameter</th>\n      <th style=\"padding:8px 12px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Spherical Mirrors</th>\n      <th style=\"padding:8px 12px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">Spherical Lenses</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px 12px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Fundamental Formula</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px 12px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">$\\frac{1}{f} = \\frac{1}{v} + \\frac{1}{u}$</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px 12px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">$\\frac{1}{f} = \\frac{1}{v} - \\frac{1}{u}$</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px 12px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Magnification ($m$)</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px 12px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">$m = -\\frac{v}{u} = \\frac{h'}{h}$</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px 12px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\">$m = +\\frac{v}{u} = \\frac{h'}{h}$</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px 12px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Converging Action</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px 12px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Concave Mirror</strong> converges light rays.</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px 12px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Convex Lens</strong> converges light rays.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px 12px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Diverging Action</strong></td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px 12px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Convex Mirror</strong> diverges light rays.</td>\n      <td style=\"padding:8px 12px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><strong>Concave Lens</strong> diverges light rays.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<p><strong>3. Numerical on Power of Combination:</strong></p>\n<p>Find the focal length and nature of a lens combination consisting of a convex lens of $+3.5\\text{ D}$ and a concave lens of $-1.5\\text{ D}$ in contact:</p>\n$$P_{\\text{net}} = P_1 + P_2 = +3.5\\text{ D} + (-1.5\\text{ D}) = +2.0\\text{ D}$$\n$$f_{\\text{net}} = \\frac{1}{P_{\\text{net}}} = \\frac{1}{+2.0\\text{ D}} = +0.5\\text{ m} = +50\\text{ cm}$$\n<p>Since the net power and focal length are positive, the combination behaves as a <strong>converging (convex) lens</strong> of focal length $50\\text{ cm}$.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Cartesian sign convention rules for mirrors and lenses</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Formulas and comparative table between mirrors and lenses</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Solving power combination problem: P = +2.0 D, f = +50 cm, converging nature</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case Study Questions (HOTS) — Chapter 9</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #34d399;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Case Study Question • Solar Concentrators & Dental Mirrors</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\">A dentist uses a small mirror to inspect cavities in the teeth of patients. Large curved mirrors are also used in solar furnaces to produce high temperatures. (i) Which type of mirror is used by dentists and why? (ii) Where should the patient's tooth be placed relative to this mirror? (iii) Which type of mirror is used in solar furnaces and where is the solar crucible placed?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(i) Dentist Mirror:</strong> A <strong>Concave mirror</strong>. When an object is placed between the pole ($P$) and principal focus ($F$) of a concave mirror, it forms an erect, virtual, and highly magnified image, enabling the dentist to inspect tiny dental fissures and tooth decay clearly.</li>\n  <li><strong>(ii) Position of Tooth:</strong> Between the <strong>pole ($P$) and the principal focus ($F$)</strong> of the mirror (within the focal length).</li>\n  <li><strong>(iii) Solar Furnace Mirror:</strong> Large <strong>Concave mirrors</strong> are used. The solar crucible or vessel containing materials to be melted is placed at the <strong>Principal Focus ($F$)</strong> of the mirror. Incoming parallel solar rays from the sun converge intensely at the focus, generating temperatures over $3000^\\circ\\text{C}$ without fossil fuels.</li>\n</ol></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(8)\">← Previous Chapter</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(10)\">Next Chapter →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "10": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch10\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">10</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">Science • Class 10 Board Exam (NCERT Rationalized)</div>\n      <h2>Chapter 10: The Human Eye and the Colourful World</h2>\n      <p>Eye Anatomy, Defects of Vision (Myopia, Hypermetropia), Dispersion, Atmospheric Refraction & Scattering — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text Questions (Page 171, 178)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch10-q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch10-q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What is meant by power of accommodation of the eye? State the near point and far point of a normal human eye.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Definition:</strong></p>\n<p>The ability of the human eye lens to adjust its focal length using the <strong>ciliary muscles</strong> so as to focus clearly on both nearby and distant objects onto the retina is called the <strong>Power of Accommodation</strong>.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Physiological Mechanism:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Viewing Distant Objects (Relaxed Eye):</strong><br>\n    When looking at objects at infinity, the ciliary muscles are completely relaxed. The eye lens becomes <strong>thin</strong>, its radius of curvature increases, and its focal length increases to its maximum value ($\\approx 2.5\\text{ cm}$, equal to eye globe diameter). Light rays converge sharply onto the retina.\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Viewing Nearby Objects (Accommodated Eye):</strong><br>\n    When viewing nearby reading material, the ciliary muscles contract. This relaxes tension on the suspensory ligaments, allowing the flexible crystalline lens to become <strong>thicker and more convex</strong>. Its focal length decreases, converging diverging rays from close objects onto the retina.\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Visual Limits:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Near Point of Normal Eye (Least distance of distinct vision, $D$):</strong> <strong>$25\\text{ cm}$</strong>. (Objects closer than 25 cm cause ocular strain and appear blurred).</li>\n      <li><strong>Far Point of Normal Eye:</strong> <strong>Infinity ($\\infty$)</strong>.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Precise definition of Power of Accommodation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Mechanism of ciliary muscles altering lens thickness for distant vs nearby objects</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Stating normal Near point (25 cm) and Far point (Infinity)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch10-q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch10-q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Differentiate between Myopia and Hypermetropia with respect to: (i) Definition, (ii) Causes, (iii) Image position, (iv) Corrective lens. What is Presbyopia?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[5 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Comparative Study of Eye Defects and Optical Corrections:</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>1. Myopia (Near-sightedness / Short-sightedness):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Description:</strong> A defective vision in which a person can see nearby objects clearly, but cannot see distant objects distinctly. The far point of the myopic eye is closer than infinity.</li>\n  <li><strong>Optical Cause:</strong>\n    <br>(a) Excessive curvature of the eye lens (lens is too thick and converges rays too strongly).\n    <br>(b) Elongation of the eyeball from front to back.\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Image Formation:</strong> Light rays from a distant object converge to form an image <strong>in front of the retina</strong> rather than on the retina.</li>\n  <li><strong>Correction:</strong> Corrected using spectacles with a <strong>Concave lens (Diverging lens)</strong> of appropriate focal length. The concave lens diverges incoming parallel rays so that they appear to come from the person's defective far point, focusing the final image precisely on the retina.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>2. Hypermetropia (Far-sightedness / Long-sightedness):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Description:</strong> A defective vision in which a person can see distant objects clearly, but cannot see nearby reading material distinctly. The near point of the hypermetropic eye recedes beyond $25\\text{ cm}$.</li>\n  <li><strong>Optical Cause:</strong>\n    <br>(a) Focal length of the eye lens is too long (lens is too thin).\n    <br>(b) Eyeball has become too short.\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Image Formation:</strong> Light rays from a nearby object focus <strong>behind the retina</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Correction:</strong> Corrected using a <strong>Convex lens (Converging lens)</strong> of appropriate focal length. The convex lens provides extra converging power to focus rays from the standard near point ($25\\text{ cm}$) sharply onto the retina.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>3. Presbyopia:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Caused by aging due to gradual weakening of ciliary muscles and diminishing flexibility of the crystalline lens. Corrected using <strong>bifocal lenses</strong> (upper portion concave for distant vision, lower portion convex for reading).</li>\n</ul>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Myopia: Definition, 2 causes (excessive curvature/eyeball elongation), image in front of retina, concave lens correction</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Hypermetropia: Definition, 2 causes (focal length too long/eyeball too short), image behind retina, convex lens correction</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Presbyopia cause and bifocal lens correction</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch10-q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch10-q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Why do stars twinkle, but planets do not? Why is the sun visible to us about 2 minutes before actual sunrise?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Atmospheric Refraction Phenomenon:</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>1. Why Stars Twinkle:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Stars are point-sized sources of light located immense light-years away from Earth.</li>\n  <li>Earth's atmosphere consists of air layers with constantly fluctuating temperatures, air currents, and densities. As height decreases, optical density of air increases, causing continuous refraction of starlight towards the normal.</li>\n  <li>Due to physical atmospheric turbulence, the optical path and effective refractive index of air along the line of sight fluctuate continuously.</li>\n  <li>Consequently, the apparent position of the star wavers slightly, and the quantity of starlight entering the pupil fluctuates from instant to instant — appearing bright at one moment and faint at the next. This causes the <strong>twinkling effect</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p><strong>2. Why Planets Do NOT Twinkle:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Planets are situated much closer to Earth and act as <strong>extended sources of light</strong> (a collection of an infinite number of point-sized sources).</li>\n  <li>Although light from individual points fluctuates, the total variation in the amount of light entering our eye from all the individual point sources averages out to zero, nullifying the twinkling effect. Hence, planets shine with a steady light.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>3. Advanced Sunrise and Delayed Sunset:</strong></p>\n<p>Due to atmospheric refraction, the sun is visible to us about <strong>2 minutes before the actual sunrise</strong> (when the sun is still below the horizon) and about <strong>2 minutes after the actual sunset</strong>. This extends daylight by approximately 4 minutes every day.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Atmospheric refraction explanation and fluctuation of starlight causing twinkling</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Explanation of why planets do not twinkle as extended light sources</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Advance sunrise and delayed sunset time difference (2 minutes each)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">0.5 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch10-q4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch10-q4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What is the Tyndall effect? Why does the clear sky appear blue? Why are danger signal lights always red?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Scattering of Light and Natural Optical Phenomena:</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>1. Tyndall Effect:</strong></p>\n<p>The phenomenon of scattering of light by colloidal or fine suspended particles in its path, making the illuminated path of the beam visible, is called the <strong>Tyndall Effect</strong> (e.g. sunlight streaming through a canopy of a dense forest or dust-filled cinema hall).</p>\n\n<div class=\"sci-diagram-wrap\" style=\"margin: 18px auto; text-align: center; max-width: 480px; background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #d1fae5; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; box-shadow: 0 2px 8px rgba(5,150,105,0.06);\">\n  <svg viewBox=\"0 0 380 210\" width=\"100%\" height=\"185\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <!-- Triangular Glass Prism -->\n  <polygon points=\"160,25 70,180 250,180\" fill=\"#f0fdf4\" stroke=\"#059669\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" opacity=\"0.75\"/>\n  <!-- White Light Beam -->\n  <line x1=\"20\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"110\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"4\"/>\n  <text x=\"35\" y=\"125\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#b45309\">White Light</text>\n  <!-- Dispersed Spectrum Inside Prism and Out to Screen -->\n  <!-- Red light (least deviated) -->\n  <line x1=\"110\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"190\" y2=\"100\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <line x1=\"190\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"340\" y2=\"80\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/>\n  <text x=\"350\" y=\"84\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ef4444\">Red (Least bent)</text>\n  <!-- Violet light (most deviated) -->\n  <line x1=\"110\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"185\" y2=\"125\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <line x1=\"185\" y1=\"125\" x2=\"340\" y2=\"165\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/>\n  <text x=\"350\" y=\"170\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#7c3aed\">Violet (Most bent)</text>\n  <text x=\"280\" y=\"125\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#047857\">V I B G Y O R</text>\n</svg>\n  <div style=\"font-size: 0.8rem; font-weight: 600; color: #065f46; margin-top: 8px;\">Figure: Dispersion of White Light through a Triangular Glass Prism into VIBGYOR Spectrum</div>\n</div>\n<p><strong>2. Why the Clear Sky Appears Blue:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Earth's atmosphere contains gas molecules ($\\text{N}_2, \\text{O}_2$) whose physical sizes are smaller than the wavelength of visible light.</li>\n  <li>According to <strong>Rayleigh's Law of Scattering</strong>, the intensity of scattered light is inversely proportional to the fourth power of its wavelength:\n    $$I \\propto \\frac{1}{\\lambda^4}$$\n  </li>\n  <li>Blue light has a much shorter wavelength ($\\approx 400\\text{ nm}$) than red light ($\\approx 700\\text{ nm}$). Blue light is scattered about <strong>16 times more strongly</strong> by atmospheric molecules than red light. The scattered blue light enters our eyes from all directions, making the sky appear blue.</li>\n  <li><em>In Space:</em> Astronauts see the sky as pitch black because there is no atmosphere to scatter sunlight.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>3. Why Danger Signals are Red:</strong></p>\n<p>Red light has the longest wavelength in the visible spectrum and is scattered least by smoke and fog particles ($I \\propto 1/\\lambda^4$). It can penetrate long atmospheric distances without attenuation, remaining distinctly visible from far away.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Tyndall effect definition and colloidal scattering example</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Rayleigh's law I proportional to 1/lambda^4 and blue sky explanation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Explanation of red colour for danger signals and black sky in outer space</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case Study Questions (HOTS) — Chapter 10</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #34d399;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Case Study Question • Rainbow Formation & Dispersion</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\">After a light rain shower on a sunny afternoon, a magnificent curved spectrum of seven colours (rainbow) is observed in the sky opposite to the sun. (i) Name the phenomenon responsible for splitting sunlight into its component colours. (ii) Which optical component acts as a natural triangular prism in the atmosphere? (iii) Name the three sequential optical processes that occur inside a raindrop to form a rainbow.</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(i) Phenomenon:</strong> <strong>Dispersion of light</strong> (splitting of white light into its spectrum of constituent wavelengths: VIBGYOR).</li>\n  <li><strong>(ii) Natural Prisms:</strong> Tiny suspended <strong>spherical raindrops</strong> present in the humid atmosphere act like miniature glass prisms.</li>\n  <li><strong>(iii) Three Sequential Optical Processes:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>1. Refraction and Dispersion:</em> Sunlight enters the raindrop and splits into seven colours because different wavelengths travel at different speeds and bend at different angles (violet bends most, red least).</li>\n      <li><em>2. Internal Reflection:</em> The dispersed light strikes the back inner surface of the raindrop and undergoes internal reflection.</li>\n      <li><em>3. Final Refraction:</em> As light emerges out of the raindrop back into the air, it refracts again, reaching the observer's eye as a rainbow with red at the top and violet at the bottom.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(9)\">← Previous Chapter</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(11)\">Next Chapter →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "11": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch11\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">11</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">Science • Class 10 Board Exam (NCERT Rationalized)</div>\n      <h2>Chapter 11: Electricity</h2>\n      <p>Ohm's Law, Resistance, Factors Affecting Resistance, Series & Parallel Combinations, Joule's Heating & Electrical Power — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text Questions (Page 183, 187, 193, 198)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch11-q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch11-q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">State Ohm's Law. Write its mathematical formula and define the SI unit of resistance (1 Ohm).</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[2 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Ohm's Law:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Statement:</strong> The electric current ($I$) flowing through a metallic conductor is directly proportional to the potential difference ($V$) applied across its ends, provided its temperature and other physical conditions remain constant.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Mathematical Formulation:</strong></p>\n$$V \\propto I \\implies V = IR \\implies R = \\frac{V}{I}$$\n<ul>\n  <li>$V$ = Potential difference across conductor (in Volts, $\\text{V}$)</li>\n  <li>$I$ = Current flowing through conductor (in Amperes, $\\text{A}$)</li>\n  <li>$R$ = Resistance of conductor (in Ohms, $\\Omega$)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>Definition of 1 Ohm ($1\\ \\Omega$):</strong></p>\n<p>If a potential difference of $1\\text{ Volt}$ across the ends of a conductor causes a current of $1\\text{ Ampere}$ to flow through it, then the resistance of the conductor is said to be <strong>$1\\text{ Ohm}$</strong> ($1\\ \\Omega = 1\\text{ V} / 1\\text{ A}$).</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Statement of Ohm's Law mentioning constant temperature</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Mathematical formula V = IR</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">0.5 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Definition of 1 Ohm resistance</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">0.5 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch11-q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch11-q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">On what factors does the resistance of a conductor depend? Write the formula relating resistance and resistivity, and state the SI unit of resistivity.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Factors on which the Resistance of a Conductor Depends:</strong></p>\n<p>The electrical resistance ($R$) of a uniform cylindrical conductor depends on four factors:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Length of the Conductor ($l$):</strong> Resistance is <strong>directly proportional</strong> to its length:\n    $$R \\propto l$$\n    Doubling the wire's length doubles its resistance.\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Area of Cross-section ($A$):</strong> Resistance is <strong>inversely proportional</strong> to its cross-sectional area:\n    $$R \\propto \\frac{1}{A}$$\n    A thick wire offers much less resistance than a thin wire of the same material.\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Nature of the Material ($\\rho$):</strong> Characterized by the specific resistivity ($\\rho$) of the material:\n    $$R = \\rho \\frac{l}{A}$$\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Temperature:</strong> For metallic conductors, electrical resistance increases with increase in temperature.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p><strong>Electrical Resistivity ($\\rho$):</strong></p>\n$$\\rho = \\frac{R \\cdot A}{l}$$\n<p>SI unit of resistivity is $\\mathbf{\\Omega \\cdot \\text{m}}$ (Ohm-metre). It is an intrinsic characteristic property of the material that does not depend on length or thickness.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Four factors: length, cross-sectional area, material nature, temperature</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Mathematical formula R = rho * (l / A)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">SI unit of resistivity (Ohm-metre)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">0.5 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch11-q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch11-q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What are the advantages of connecting electrical devices in parallel with the battery rather than in series in domestic circuits?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Why Domestic Electrical Appliances are Connected in Parallel, NOT in Series:</strong></p>\n\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>1. Independent Operation (No Global Failure):</strong><br>\n    In a series circuit, if one appliance burns out, gets damaged, or is turned off, the single continuous circuit is broken, and <strong>all other appliances in the house turn off immediately</strong>. In a parallel circuit, each branch is independent; if one bulb fuses, other rooms remain fully illuminated.\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>2. Independent Switches:</strong><br>\n    In series, all appliances share a single switch. In parallel circuits, every individual device (fan, light, AC, TV) can have its own independent switch.\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>3. Constant Voltage for Each Appliance:</strong><br>\n    In parallel, each appliance receives the full domestic supply voltage ($220\\text{ V}$ in India) across its terminals, operating at its full rated efficiency. In series, the $220\\text{ V}$ divides across all devices, causing lights to dim and appliances to underperform.\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>4. Different Current Requirements:</strong><br>\n    Different electrical appliances have vastly different power ratings and current requirements (e.g. an electric heater draws $10\\text{ A}$, while an LED bulb requires only $0.05\\text{ A}$). In series, current is identical through all components ($I = \\text{constant}$), which is completely incompatible. Parallel circuits provide separate current paths matching each appliance's impedance.\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Independent operation: circuit break in one does not shut off others</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Full supply voltage (220 V) across each parallel branch</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Separate switches and accommodating different current demands (heater vs bulb)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch11-q4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch11-q4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Three resistors of $5\\ \\Omega, 10\\ \\Omega$ and $30\\ \\Omega$ are connected (i) in series, (ii) in parallel to a 12 V battery. Find the equivalent resistance and total current drawn in each case.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Step-by-step Circuit Numerical Solution:</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>1. Resistors in Series:</strong></p>\n<p>When resistors $R_1 = 5\\ \\Omega, R_2 = 10\\ \\Omega, R_3 = 30\\ \\Omega$ are connected in series:</p>\n$$R_s = R_1 + R_2 + R_3 = 5 + 10 + 30 = \\mathbf{45\\ \\Omega}$$\n<p>The equivalent resistance is maximum in series.</p>\n\n<p><strong>2. Resistors in Parallel:</strong></p>\n<p>When connected in parallel across a $12\\text{ V}$ battery:</p>\n$$\\frac{1}{R_p} = \\frac{1}{R_1} + \\frac{1}{R_2} + \\frac{1}{R_3} = \\frac{1}{5} + \\frac{1}{10} + \\frac{1}{30}$$\n<p>LCM of 5, 10, 30 is 30:</p>\n$$\\frac{1}{R_p} = \\frac{6 + 3 + 1}{30} = \\frac{10}{30} = \\frac{1}{3} \\implies R_p = \\mathbf{3\\ \\Omega}$$\n\n<div class=\"sci-diagram-wrap\" style=\"margin: 18px auto; text-align: center; max-width: 480px; background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #d1fae5; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; box-shadow: 0 2px 8px rgba(5,150,105,0.06);\">\n  <svg viewBox=\"0 0 380 200\" width=\"100%\" height=\"175\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <!-- Parallel branches -->\n  <line x1=\"40\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"90\" y2=\"100\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <line x1=\"90\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"90\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <!-- Branch 1 (R1) -->\n  <line x1=\"90\" y1=\"50\" x2=\"130\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <rect x=\"130\" y=\"42\" width=\"60\" height=\"16\" fill=\"#ecfdf5\" stroke=\"#059669\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"160\" y=\"54\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#047857\" text-anchor=\"middle\">R₁ = 5 Ω</text>\n  <line x1=\"190\" y1=\"50\" x2=\"230\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <!-- Branch 2 (R2) -->\n  <line x1=\"90\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"130\" y2=\"100\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <rect x=\"130\" y=\"92\" width=\"60\" height=\"16\" fill=\"#ecfdf5\" stroke=\"#059669\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"160\" y=\"104\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#047857\" text-anchor=\"middle\">R₂ = 10 Ω</text>\n  <line x1=\"190\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"230\" y2=\"100\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <!-- Branch 3 (R3) -->\n  <line x1=\"90\" y1=\"150\" x2=\"130\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <rect x=\"130\" y=\"142\" width=\"60\" height=\"16\" fill=\"#ecfdf5\" stroke=\"#059669\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"160\" y=\"154\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#047857\" text-anchor=\"middle\">R₃ = 30 Ω</text>\n  <line x1=\"190\" y1=\"150\" x2=\"230\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <line x1=\"230\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"230\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <!-- Battery and Ammeter -->\n  <line x1=\"230\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"340\" y2=\"100\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <line x1=\"340\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"340\" y2=\"190\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <line x1=\"340\" y1=\"190\" x2=\"40\" y2=\"190\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <line x1=\"40\" y1=\"190\" x2=\"40\" y2=\"100\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <!-- Battery 12 V -->\n  <line x1=\"180\" y1=\"180\" x2=\"180\" y2=\"200\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n  <line x1=\"190\" y1=\"184\" x2=\"190\" y2=\"196\" stroke=\"#0f172a\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n  <text x=\"185\" y=\"174\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#dc2626\" text-anchor=\"middle\">12 V</text>\n</svg>\n  <div style=\"font-size: 0.8rem; font-weight: 600; color: #065f46; margin-top: 8px;\">Figure: Parallel Electrical Circuit with Three Resistors and a 12 V Battery Source</div>\n</div>\n<p><strong>3. Total Current from 12 V Battery in Parallel:</strong></p>\n$$I_{\\text{total}} = \\frac{V}{R_p} = \\frac{12\\text{ V}}{3\\ \\Omega} = \\mathbf{4\\text{ A}}$$\n\n<p><strong>4. Branch Currents:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>$I_1 = \\frac{V}{R_1} = \\frac{12}{5} = 2.4\\text{ A}$</li>\n  <li>$I_2 = \\frac{V}{R_2} = \\frac{12}{10} = 1.2\\text{ A}$</li>\n  <li>$I_3 = \\frac{V}{R_3} = \\frac{12}{30} = 0.4\\text{ A}$</li>\n  <li><em>Check:</em> $I_1 + I_2 + I_3 = 2.4 + 1.2 + 0.4 = 4.0\\text{ A}$. (Verified)</li>\n</ul>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Calculating series equivalent resistance = 45 Ohms</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Calculating parallel equivalent resistance = 3 Ohms</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Calculating total current = 4 A and branch currents</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch11-q5\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch11-q5')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 5</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">State Joule's Law of Heating and describe three practical applications. An electric refrigerator rated 400 W operates 8 hours/day and an iron of 1000 W operates 2 hours/day. Calculate the total electricity bill for 30 days at Rs 5.00 per unit (kWh).</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[5 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Joule's Law of Heating and Electric Power Calculations:</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>1. Joule's Law of Heating:</strong></p>\n<p>When an electric current $I$ flows through a resistor $R$ for time $t$, the electrical energy consumed is dissipated entirely as heat energy ($H$).</p>\n$$\\mathbf{H = I^2 R t}$$\n<p><strong>Three Factors:</strong> Heat produced in a resistor is:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Directly proportional to the <strong>square of current</strong> ($H \\propto I^2$).</li>\n  <li>Directly proportional to the <strong>resistance</strong> for a given current ($H \\propto R$).</li>\n  <li>Directly proportional to the <strong>time</strong> for which current flows ($H \\propto t$).</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p><strong>2. Practical Applications:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Heating Appliances:</em> Electric irons, toasters, and geysers use heating elements made of <strong>Nichrome</strong> (alloy of Ni, Cr, Mn, Fe) because nichrome has very high resistivity and does not oxidise (burn) even at red-hot temperatures ($800^\\circ\\text{C}$).</li>\n  <li><em>Electric Bulbs:</em> Filaments are made of <strong>Tungsten</strong> due to its extraordinarily high melting point ($3422^\\circ\\text{C}$).</li>\n  <li><em>Electric Fuse:</em> Made of a lead-tin alloy with low melting point to melt and break circuit during overcurrent.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>3. Electrical Energy Commercial Unit Problem:</strong></p>\n<p>An electric refrigerator rated $400\\text{ W}$ operates 8 hours/day. An electric iron rated $1000\\text{ W}$ operates 2 hours/day. What is the cost of energy to operate them for 30 days at Rs 5.00 per kWh?</p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Energy consumed by refrigerator in 30 days:\n    $$E_1 = P_1 \\times t_1 \\times 30 = 400\\text{ W} \\times 8\\text{ h} \\times 30 = 96,000\\text{ Wh} = 96\\text{ kWh}$$\n  </li>\n  <li>Energy consumed by electric iron in 30 days:\n    $$E_2 = P_2 \\times t_2 \\times 30 = 1000\\text{ W} \\times 2\\text{ h} \\times 30 = 60,000\\text{ Wh} = 60\\text{ kWh}$$\n  </li>\n  <li>Total energy consumed $= 96 + 60 = 156\\text{ kWh (units)}$.</li>\n  <li>Total cost $= 156\\text{ units} \\times \\text{Rs } 5.00/\\text{unit} = \\mathbf{\\text{Rs } 780.00}$.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Statement and formula of Joule's Law of Heating H = I^2 R t</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Explaining practical heating applications (Nichrome, Tungsten, Fuse)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Solving electricity commercial billing problem correctly to find Rs 780.00</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case Study Questions (HOTS) — Chapter 11</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #34d399;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Case Study Question • Short Circuit & Earthing Protection</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\">A resident plugged a high-power immersion heater into a domestic socket. Suddenly a spark occurred, the fuse wire melted, and the house lights went off. (i) Differentiate between short-circuiting and overloading. (ii) What is the function of an earth wire in metallic appliances? (iii) Why is fuse always connected in the live wire?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(i) Short-circuiting vs Overloading:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Short-circuiting:</em> Occurs when the live wire and neutral wire come in direct physical contact due to faulty insulation. The resistance of the circuit drops near zero, and an enormous, dangerous surge of current flows instantly.</li>\n      <li><em>Overloading:</em> Occurs when too many electrical appliances of high power ratings are switched on simultaneously across a single socket, drawing current beyond the safe current-carrying capacity of domestic wires.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(ii) Function of Earth Wire:</strong> The green/yellow earth wire is connected to a metal plate buried deep in the ground. It connects to the metallic casing of heavy appliances (refrigerator, toaster, iron). If an insulation fault makes the metal body live, the current safely drains into the earth through the low-resistance path, tripping the fuse and protecting the user from a lethal electric shock.</li>\n  <li><strong>(iii) Fuse in Live Wire:</strong> If the fuse is placed in the neutral wire and blows, the appliance remains connected to the high-voltage live wire ($220\\text{ V}$), posing a fatal shock risk if touched. Placing it in the live wire completely isolates the appliance from the electrical mains upon blowing.</li>\n</ol></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(10)\">← Previous Chapter</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(12)\">Next Chapter →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "12": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch12\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">12</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">Science • Class 10 Board Exam (NCERT Rationalized)</div>\n      <h2>Chapter 12: Magnetic Effects of Electric Current</h2>\n      <p>Magnetic Field Lines, Right-Hand Thumb Rule, Solenoid, Fleming's Left-Hand Rule & Domestic Electric Circuits — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text Questions (Page 205, 209, 214)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch12-q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch12-q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">List the important properties of magnetic field lines. Why don't two magnetic field lines intersect each other?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Properties of Magnetic Field Lines:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Direction of Field Lines:</strong> Outside a bar magnet, magnetic field lines emerge from the <strong>North pole</strong> and enter the <strong>South pole</strong>. Inside the magnet, the direction is from the South pole to the North pole. Thus, magnetic field lines form <strong>continuous closed loops</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Relative Strength (Density):</strong> The degree of closeness (crowding) of the field lines is a measure of the strength of the magnetic field. The field is strongest near the poles where lines are crowded.</li>\n  <li><strong>Non-Intersecting Nature:</strong> <strong>No two magnetic field lines ever intersect each other.</strong> If they did intersect at a point, a compass needle placed at that intersection point would have to point in two different directions simultaneously, which is physically impossible.</li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Continuous closed loops from North to South outside magnet</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Density indicates magnetic field strength</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">0.5 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Proof why two magnetic field lines never intersect</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch12-q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch12-q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">State Maxwell's Right-Hand Thumb Rule. What is a solenoid? Describe the nature of the magnetic field inside a current-carrying solenoid.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[4 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Right-Hand Thumb Rule and Solenoid Magnetic Field:</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>1. Maxwell's Right-Hand Thumb Rule:</strong></p>\n<p>Imagine holding a straight current-carrying conductor in your right hand such that your outstretched <strong>thumb points in the direction of current</strong>. Then your <strong>curled fingers encircling the wire point in the direction of the concentric magnetic field lines</strong>.</p>\n\n<p><strong>2. Magnetic Field of a Solenoid:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>A <strong>Solenoid</strong> is a long coil containing many circular turns of insulated copper wire wrapped closely in the shape of a cylinder.</li>\n  <li>When electric current passes through the solenoid, it produces a magnetic field that is identical to that of a <strong>bar magnet</strong>. One end of the solenoid behaves as a magnetic North pole and the other end as a South pole.</li>\n  <li>Inside the core of the solenoid, the magnetic field lines are <strong>parallel straight lines</strong>. This indicates that the magnetic field is completely <strong>uniform</strong> at all points inside the solenoid.</li>\n  <li><em>Electromagnet:</em> When a soft iron rod is placed inside the solenoid core, the strong magnetic field magnetizes the iron, forming a powerful temporary <strong>electromagnet</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Statement of Maxwell's Right-Hand Thumb Rule</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Solenoid definition and similarity of field to a bar magnet</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Parallel lines inside solenoid proving uniform field and electromagnet creation</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch12-q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch12-q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">State Fleming's Left-Hand Rule. When is the force experienced by a current-carrying conductor placed in a magnetic field maximum?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Fleming's Left-Hand Rule:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Statement:</strong> Stretch the thumb, forefinger, and middle finger of your left hand mutually perpendicular to each other such that:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li>The <strong>Forefinger</strong> points in the direction of the <strong>Magnetic Field ($\\vec{B}$)</strong>.</li>\n  <li>The <strong>Middle finger</strong> points in the direction of the <strong>Electric Current ($\\vec{I}$)</strong>.</li>\n  <li>Then the <strong>Thumb</strong> points in the direction of the <strong>Mechanical Force / Motion ($\\vec{F}$)</strong> acting on the conductor.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p><strong>Condition for Maximum Force:</strong></p>\n<p>The mechanical force experienced by a conductor of length $l$ carrying current $I$ in a magnetic field $B$ is given by $F = I l B \\sin\\theta$.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>The force is <strong>maximum</strong> when the conductor is oriented <strong>perpendicular ($90^\\circ$)</strong> to the magnetic field ($\\sin 90^\\circ = 1$).</li>\n  <li>The force is <strong>zero</strong> when the conductor is parallel ($0^\\circ$) to the magnetic field ($\\sin 0^\\circ = 0$).</li>\n</ul>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Statement of Fleming's Left-Hand Rule (Thumb-Force, Forefinger-Field, Middle-Current)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Condition for maximum force: conductor perpendicular to magnetic field (theta = 90 deg)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch12-q4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch12-q4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">Describe the three wires used in domestic electric circuits with their color codes and potential differences. Explain why two separate circuits (5 A and 15 A) are used in houses.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[5 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Domestic Electric Supply Circuits:</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>1. Three Types of Wires in Domestic Circuits:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Live Wire (Phase wire, usually Red / Brown insulation):</strong> Carries current from the power station at a high electric potential of <strong>$220\\text{ V}$</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Neutral Wire (Negative wire, usually Black / Blue insulation):</strong> Completes the circuit back to the generator at zero potential (<strong>$0\\text{ V}$</strong>). The potential difference between live and neutral wires in India is $220\\text{ V} - 0\\text{ V} = 220\\text{ V}$ AC ($50\\text{ Hz}$).</li>\n  <li><strong>Earth Wire (Grounding wire, usually Green / Yellow insulation):</strong> A safety wire connected to a copper plate buried deep underground; carries zero potential under normal operation.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p><strong>2. Two Independent Circuits in Domestic Households:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>15 A Power Circuit:</strong> For high power-rating heating appliances (air conditioners, geysers, refrigerators, washing machines).</li>\n  <li><strong>5 A Lighting Circuit:</strong> For low power-rating appliances (fans, LED tube lights, television, laptops).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>3. Electrical Safety Devices:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Electric Fuse / MCB (Miniature Circuit Breaker):</strong> Protects circuits and household appliances from damage caused by overloading and short circuits by automatically breaking current flow when threshold exceeds rated capacity.</li>\n  <li><strong>Earthing:</strong> Connects metallic bodies of appliances to earth, preventing severe electric shocks.</li>\n</ul>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Description and color codes of Live wire (220 V), Neutral wire (0 V), Earth wire</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">2 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Two circuits in domestic wiring: 15 A power line and 5 A lighting line</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Role of fuse/MCB and earthing in human safety and appliance protection</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case Study Questions (HOTS) — Chapter 12</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #34d399;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Case Study Question • AC vs DC in Long-Distance Power Transmission</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\">Electricity generated at hydroelectric or thermal power stations is transmitted over hundreds of kilometers to cities using Alternating Current (AC). (i) What is the fundamental difference between Alternating Current (AC) and Direct Current (DC)? (ii) What is the frequency of domestic AC supply in India? (iii) State one major advantage of AC over DC in power transmission.</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(i) AC vs DC:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Direct Current (DC):</strong> Electric current flows in a single constant unidirectional path without reversing polarity (e.g. from dry cells/batteries).</li>\n      <li><strong>Alternating Current (AC):</strong> Electric current reverses its direction of flow periodically at regular intervals of time.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>(ii) AC Frequency in India:</strong> <strong>$50\\text{ Hz}$</strong> (50 cycles per second). The current changes direction every $\\frac{1}{100}\\text{ second}$ (100 reversals per second).</li>\n  <li><strong>(iii) Major Advantage of AC:</strong> Alternating current can be stepped up to extraordinarily high voltages (e.g. $132\\text{ kV}, 400\\text{ kV}$) using step-up transformers. Because power loss in transmission lines is $P_{\\text{loss}} = I^2 R$, transmitting at high voltage dramatically reduces current ($I$), enabling electric power to be transmitted over hundreds of kilometers with <strong>negligible Joule heating loss</strong>. DC cannot be transformed easily.</li>\n</ol></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(11)\">← Previous Chapter</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn next\" onclick=\"showChapter(13)\">Next Chapter →</button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "13": "<section class=\"chapter-section\" id=\"ch13\">\n  <div class=\"chapter-header\">\n    <div class=\"ch-badge\">13</div>\n    <div class=\"chapter-header-info\">\n      <div class=\"ch-category\">Science • Class 10 Board Exam (NCERT Rationalized)</div>\n      <h2>Chapter 13: Our Environment</h2>\n      <p>Ecosystem, Trophic Levels, 10% Energy Transfer Law, Biomagnification & Ozone Depletion — CBSE Board Marking Scheme 2026-27</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"ex-div\">NCERT In-Text Questions (Page 219, 222)</div>\n\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch13-q1\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch13-q1')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 1</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What are biodegradable and non-biodegradable substances? Give two examples of each. How do they affect the environment?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Comparison: Biodegradable vs Non-biodegradable Substances</strong></p>\n\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Biodegradable Substances:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Substances that can be broken down into simpler, non-toxic harmless substances by the biological enzymatic action of <strong>microorganisms (saprophytic bacteria and fungi)</strong> over a reasonable period.</li>\n      <li>They originate from plant and animal sources.</li>\n      <li><em>Examples:</em> Fruit and vegetable peels, paper, cotton cloth, cow dung, agricultural wastes.</li>\n      <li><em>Environmental Impact:</em> If accumulated excessively, they produce foul odor and breed disease vectors, but otherwise recycle naturally into soil humus.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Non-biodegradable Substances:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Substances that <strong>cannot be broken down or degraded</strong> by physical or microbial enzymatic action; they persist in the biosphere unchanged for hundreds of years.</li>\n      <li>They are synthetic man-made compounds.</li>\n      <li><em>Examples:</em> Plastics, polythene bags, DDT, heavy metals (mercury, lead, cadmium), synthetic pesticides.</li>\n      <li><em>Environmental Impact:</em> Choke drainage systems, poison soil and groundwater, harm grazing cattle, and undergo dangerous <strong>Biological Magnification</strong> across food chains.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Definition and examples of biodegradable substances</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Definition and examples of non-biodegradable substances and persistence</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch13-q2\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch13-q2')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 2</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What are trophic levels? Give an example of a food chain and state the different trophic levels in it. Explain the 10% Law of energy flow and why food chains are limited to 3-4 steps.</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[4 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Trophic Levels and the 10% Law of Energy Transfer:</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>1. What are Trophic Levels?</strong></p>\n<p>Each distinct feeding step or level in a food chain where transfer of food energy takes place is called a <strong>Trophic Level</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>First Trophic Level ($T_1$):</strong> <em>Producers</em> (Green plants synthesizing food via photosynthesis).</li>\n  <li><strong>Second Trophic Level ($T_2$):</strong> <em>Primary Consumers / Herbivores</em> (Deer, grasshopper, cow).</li>\n  <li><strong>Third Trophic Level ($T_3$):</strong> <em>Secondary Consumers / Small Carnivores</em> (Frog, fox, snake).</li>\n  <li><strong>Fourth Trophic Level ($T_4$):</strong> <em>Tertiary Consumers / Top Carnivores</em> (Lion, eagle, tiger).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>2. The 10% Law (Lindeman's Law of Trophic Efficiency):</strong></p>\n<p>Only about <strong>$10\\%$ of the energy</strong> entering a particular trophic level is available for transfer to the next trophic level. The remaining <strong>$90\\%$</strong> is lost to the environment as metabolic heat during cellular respiration, digestion, locomotion, growth, and reproduction.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Numerical Example:</strong></p>\n<p>If $20,000\\text{ Joules}$ of solar energy falls on green plants, plants capture only $1\\%$ ($200\\text{ J}$):</p>\n$$\\text{Producers (Plants): } 200\\text{ J}$$\n$$\\text{Herbivore (Deer): } 10\\% \\text{ of } 200\\text{ J} = 20\\text{ J}$$\n$$\\text{Carnivore (Lion): } 10\\% \\text{ of } 20\\text{ J} = \\mathbf{2\\text{ J}}$$\n<p><em>Consequence:</em> Because energy diminishes drastically at each level, food chains rarely exceed <strong>3 to 4 trophic levels</strong>, beyond which the available energy is insufficient to sustain viable consumer populations.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Definition and classification of the 4 trophic levels</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Statement of 10% law and numerical calculation (200 J -> 20 J -> 2 J)</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Reason why food chains are restricted to 3-4 trophic levels</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch13-q3\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch13-q3')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 3</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What is biological magnification? Will the levels of this magnification be different at different levels of the ecosystem? In which trophic level is it maximum?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[3 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>Biological Magnification (Biomagnification):</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>1. Definition:</strong></p>\n<p>The progressive increase in the concentration of toxic, non-biodegradable chemical pollutants (such as synthetic pesticides, DDT, heavy metals) per unit weight of organisms at <strong>successively higher trophic levels</strong> in a food chain is called <strong>Biological Magnification</strong>.</p>\n\n<p><strong>2. How Biomagnification Occurs:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Non-biodegradable pesticides like DDT are sprayed on agricultural crops to protect them from insect pests.</li>\n  <li>These chemical residues are washed by rainwater into soils and water bodies (ponds, lakes, rivers).</li>\n  <li>They are absorbed by aquatic plants (phytoplankton) along with water and minerals. Because these chemicals are non-biodegradable and fat-soluble, they cannot be metabolized or excreted by organisms and accumulate in fatty tissues.</li>\n  <li>When zooplankton eat phytoplankton, small fish eat zooplankton, large fish eat small fish, and fish-eating birds or humans consume large fish, the concentration of DDT multiplies progressively at each ascending trophic level.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p><strong>3. Maximum Accumulation in Humans:</strong></p>\n<p>Since <strong>human beings occupy the apex (top) of multiple terrestrial and aquatic food chains</strong>, the highest concentration of these toxic, carcinogenic chemicals accumulates in human tissues, causing hormonal disruption, neurological disorders, and cancer.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Precise definition of Biological Magnification</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Step-by-step mechanism of bioaccumulation through food chain steps</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Justifying why top consumers/humans accumulate the highest concentrations</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"q-card\" id=\"c10s-ch13-q4\">\n    <div class=\"q-head\" onclick=\"toggleQ('c10s-ch13-q4')\">\n      <div class=\"q-num\">Question 4</div>\n      <div class=\"q-text\">What is ozone and how does it affect any ecosystem? Write the photochemical equations for its formation. How is ozone depleted, and what steps have been taken by UNEP to limit this damage?</div>\n      <div class=\"q-marks\">[5 Marks]</div>\n      <div class=\"q-toggle\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"19\"/><line x1=\"5\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"19\" y2=\"12\"/></svg></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"q-answer\">\n      <div class=\"answer-box\">\n        <div class=\"answer-label\">✅ CBSE Board Standard Step-by-Step Solution</div>\n        <div class=\"answer-text\">\n<p><strong>The Ozone Layer and its Environmental Depletion:</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>1. Nature and Formation of Ozone ($\\text{O}_3$):</strong></p>\n<p>Ozone is a triatomic molecule made of three oxygen atoms ($\\text{O}_3$). While ozone is a deadly poison at ground level, in the <strong>Stratosphere</strong> (15 to 30 km above Earth) it forms a vital protective shield that filters out lethal <strong>ultraviolet (UV) radiation</strong> emitted by the sun.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Photochemical Formation of Ozone in Stratosphere:</strong></p>\n$$\\text{O}_2 \\xrightarrow{\\text{High-energy UV}} \\text{O} + \\text{O} \\quad \\text{(Splitting of oxygen molecule into nascent oxygen)}$$\n$$\\text{O} + \\text{O}_2 \\longrightarrow \\text{O}_3 \\quad \\text{(Formation of Ozone)}$$\n\n<p><strong>2. Biological Importance of Ozone Shield:</strong></p>\n<p>High-energy UV-B radiation is severely harmful to life on Earth. Without the stratospheric ozone layer, UV radiation reaching Earth would cause:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Skin cancer and melanoma in humans.</li>\n  <li>Severe cataracts and permanent damage to eyes.</li>\n  <li>Suppression of the human immune system.</li>\n  <li>Destruction of marine phytoplankton, causing collapse of oceanic food chains.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>3. Cause of Ozone Depletion:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>The depletion of the ozone layer was primarily caused by synthetic chemicals called <strong>Chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs)</strong>, widely used as refrigerants in refrigerators, air conditioners, aerosol propellants, and fire extinguishers.</li>\n  <li>In the stratosphere, UV rays split CFCs, releasing highly reactive free chlorine atoms ($\\text{Cl}^\\bullet$). A single chlorine radical can catalytically destroy over <strong>100,000 ozone molecules</strong> before being neutralized.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>4. International Corrective Action — Montreal Protocol (1987):</strong></p>\n<p>In 1987, the United Nations Environment Programme (UNEP) succeeded in forging an international treaty called the <strong>Montreal Protocol</strong>, legally freezing and subsequently phasing out the global production and use of CFCs, mandating CFC-free refrigerators worldwide.</p>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"marking-scheme\">\n          <div class=\"marking-title\">CBSE Board Marking Scheme Breakdown 2026-27</div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Ozone definition and balanced photochemical formation equations</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Importance of ozone shield in protecting against skin cancer, cataract, phytoplankton destruction</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1.5 Marks</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">Role of Chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs) and free chlorine radicals in ozone destruction</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n          <div class=\"marking-row\"><span class=\"marking-key\">UNEP Montreal Protocol (1987) international agreement banning CFCs</span><span class=\"marking-marks\">1 Mark</span></div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"cbq-section\">\n    <div class=\"cbq-header\"><span>🎯 Competency &amp; Case Study Questions (HOTS) — Chapter 13</span><span class=\"cbq-badge\">CBSE Board 2026-27</span></div>\n    <div class=\"cbq-body\">\n      <div class=\"cbq-card\" style=\"border-left:4px solid #34d399;\">\n        <div class=\"cbq-type\" style=\"color:#059669;\">Case Study Question • Kulhad vs Plastic Cups on Railways</div>\n        <div class=\"cbq-question\">A few years ago, disposable plastic cups were banned on Indian trains and replaced by clay cups (kulhads). Later, disposable paper cups were introduced. (i) Why were disposable plastic cups banned? (ii) What was the environmental problem caused by mass-scale use of kulhads? (iii) Why are disposable paper cups considered a better alternative?</div>\n        <button class=\"cbq-show-btn\" onclick=\"toggleCBQ(this)\">▶ Show Answer</button>\n        <div class=\"cbq-answer\"><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>(i) Reason for Plastic Cup Ban:</strong> Plastic cups are non-biodegradable. Billions of discarded cups littered railway tracks, choked storm water drainage systems, poisoned soil, released toxic dioxins when burned, and were ingested by stray cows causing fatal intestinal blockages.</li>\n  <li><strong>(ii) Problem with Kulhads:</strong> Making billions of clay kulhads daily required digging up massive quantities of rich, fertile agricultural <strong>topsoil</strong>. This caused severe depletion of topsoil, land degradation, and reduced agricultural fertility, alongside air pollution from brick kilns.</li>\n  <li><strong>(iii) Advantage of Paper Cups:</strong> Disposable paper cups are manufactured from renewable plantation wood pulp. They are completely <strong>biodegradable</strong>, decompose naturally within a few weeks without polluting soil or choking drains, and can be recycled efficiently without stripping fertile topsoil.</li>\n</ol></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"chapter-nav-btns\">\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"showChapter(12)\">← Previous Chapter</button>\n    <button class=\"ch-nav-btn\" onclick=\"window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'})\">↑ Back to Top</button>\n    <div></div>\n  </div>\n</section>\n"
};
